window.TIKO_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"index.html","t":"Tiko Editor Documentation","s":"Welcome","k":"home start documentation help tiko editor freebasic","b":"Everything you need to install, learn and master Tiko — the native Windows code editor for FreeBASIC development. Tiko is a fast, native Windows code editor built for FreeBASIC development — with a project system, an integrated compiler workflow, a real debugger and a symbol engine that understands your code. It edits any text-based language equally well. Start the 25-minute Quick Start What is Tiko? Full tutorial Tiko editing a FreeBASIC project. The Explorer sits on the left, the editor fills the centre, and the Output panel is docked below. Start here Quick Start Install, open a file, edit, search, build and run — in about 25 minutes. What is Tiko? The product in a page: what it does, who it is for, and its design philosophy. Ten-lesson tutorial A progressive course from your first file through to advanced editing. Tour the interface Every part of the main window, named and explained with diagrams. Explore the documentation Editing text Cursors, selections, clipboard, indentation, encoding and folding. Searching Find, Replace, Find in Project, regular expressions and search history. Navigation Goto Definition, symbol search, bookmarks, function list and history. Productivity Autocomplete, code tips, the formatter, and line and block commands. Projects The workspace model, the Explorer, project files and project options. Building programs Compiler setup, build configurations, running, and error navigation. Debugging Breakpoints, stepping, watches, the call stack and the debugger panes. Customization Themes, fonts, syntax colours, keyboard shortcuts, layout and settings. Reference Keyboard shortcuts Every default shortcut, grouped by category, in printable tables. Menu reference Every menu and every command, with shortcuts and related topics. Configuration reference Every setting: purpose, default, accepted values and related options. Command reference Commands with their identifiers, menu locations and shortcuts. Dialog reference Every dialog box and what each control does. FAQ Around fifty common questions with short, direct answers. Tips and tricks Power-user workflows, hidden features and keyboard and mouse tricks. Troubleshooting Fixes for start-up failures, compiler problems, encoding and performance. Glossary Plain-language definitions of the terms used throughout these pages. Index A complete alphabetical index of topics and features. How to use thi"},{"u":"what-is-tiko.html","t":"What is Tiko?","s":"Welcome","k":"about overview introduction philosophy requirements freebasic scintilla gpl planetsquires paul squires native win32","b":"An overview of the editor: what it does, why you might choose it, its design philosophy, and what it needs to run. What is Tiko? Tiko is a programmer's code editor for Microsoft Windows, written by Paul Squires (PlanetSquires Software) and released under the GNU General Public License v3. It is designed first and foremost for FreeBASIC development, and it ships with the pieces that make that work end to end: a project system, compiler integration, a build configuration manager, a source-level debugger and a symbol engine that reads your code as you type. It is not limited to FreeBASIC. Tiko is a competent general-purpose text and code editor, so C, C++, resource scripts, INI files, plain text and anything else you point it at open and edit normally. Tiko is a native Win32 application . There is no runtime to install, no browser engine behind the editor surface, and no background service. The editing surface is built on Scintilla , the same proven editing component used by Notepad++ and SciTE. Why use Tiko? It is fast, and stays fast Tiko starts quickly and keeps responding on large files. Source parsing happens on a background worker thread and its results are handed back to the user interface, so scanning a big project never blocks your typing. It understands FreeBASIC specifically Generic editors treat BASIC as coloured text. Tiko parses it. The symbol engine extracts procedures and their parameters, types, enumerations and variables — with the file, line and column of each — which is what powers Goto Definition, the function list, code tips and autocomplete. The whole build cycle lives in one window Set up your compiler once, then edit, compile, run and debug without leaving the editor. Compiler errors appear in the Output panel; clicking one jumps to the offending line. It is genuinely configurable Fourteen bundled themes, a full theme editor, complete keyboard remapping, a code formatter with per-rule control, user-defined external tools and six interface languages — all stored in plain files next to the executable. Key features Area What you get Editing Scintilla editing surface, syntax highlighting, code folding, brace matching, multiple selections, column selection, bookmarks, split views and zoom. Code intelligence Autocomplete, code tips (parameter hints), Goto Definition ( F12 ), symbol search ( Ctrl + P ), a live function list, and back/forward n"},{"u":"quick-start.html","t":"Quick Start","s":"Welcome","k":"quick start getting started install launch new file save open build run compile debug tutorial beginner first steps","b":"Install Tiko, learn the interface, and work through editing, searching, building, running and debugging a program — in about 25 minutes. Note This walkthrough takes 20–30 minutes and touches every part of the editor you will use daily. If you would rather learn in smaller pieces with exercises, take the ten-lesson tutorial instead — it covers the same ground more slowly. 1. Install and launch Tiko does not use an installer. Unpack the distribution into any folder you can write to and run tiko.exe from there. Unpack the archive into a folder — for example C:\\tiko . Avoid C:\\Program Files : Tiko writes its settings beside the executable, and that location is protected. Double-click tiko.exe . The main window opens with an empty untitled workspace. Warning Run tiko.exe from the folder you unpacked, not from a copy you moved elsewhere on its own. Tiko finds its themes, settings and keyword files relative to its own location, so a stray copy starts with no theme and no configuration. 2. Get your bearings The window has five regions. Spend a moment locating each one. File Edit Search View Project Compile Debug Help 1 icons 2 Explorer project files functions bookmarks 3 main.bas × utils.bi × 4 1 ' editor surface 2 Print \"Hello, world!\" 3 Sleep 5 Compiler Search results TODO Notes Build succeeded. 6 Ln 2, Col 24 INS UTF-8 CRLF FreeBASIC 7 The Tiko main window. 1 Menu bar · 2 Panel icon strip · 3 Side panel (Explorer, Functions, Bookmarks) · 4 Document tabs · 5 Editor surface · 6 Output panel · 7 Status bar. Tip Two shortcuts worth learning immediately: Ctrl + B toggles the side panel and Ctrl + F9 toggles the Output panel. Both give the editor the whole window when you need room. 3. Create your first file Choose File › New or press Ctrl + N . A new untitled document opens in a new tab. Type the short program below. Notice that keywords colour themselves as you type, and that pressing Enter after For automatically indents the next line. fb hello.bas Copy ' hello.bas - a first FreeBASIC program Dim As Integer i For i = 1 To 5 Print \"Line \"; i Next i Print \"Press any key to finish.\" Sleep 4. Save, and Save As Press Ctrl + S . Because the file has never been saved, the Save As dialog opens. Choose a folder, name the file hello.bas , and save. The tab caption changes from Untitled to the file name. Type another line, then press Ctrl + S again. This time it saves silently"},{"u":"main-window.html","t":"The main window","s":"User Interface","k":"main window interface layout regions panels splitter resize ui tour","b":"A guided tour of Tiko's interface: the seven regions of the main window, how to resize them, and how the layout is remembered. The Tiko main window in a light theme, with the Explorer open on the left and the Output panel docked below the editor. Anatomy of the window Tiko's window is built from seven regions. Everything else — dialogs, popups, tooltips — appears on top of these. Region Purpose Show / hide Menu bar Every command in the application, grouped into eight menus. Always visible Panel icon strip Buttons that switch the side panel and run common commands such as Save, Find, Compile and Debug. With the side panel Side panel Hosts the Explorer, the function list and the bookmarks list. Ctrl + B Document tabs One tab per open file, with a close button and a tab list. Always visible Editor surface Where you edit. Can be split into two views. Always visible Output panel Compiler results, search results, TODO items and Notes. Ctrl + F9 Status bar Caret position, insert mode, encoding, line endings and language. Always visible Resizing and layout The side panel and the Output panel are separated from the editor by draggable splitter bars. Point at a splitter, and the cursor changes to a resize arrow; drag to change the split. Drag the vertical splitter to widen or narrow the side panel. Drag the horizontal splitter to change the height of the Output panel. Double-click the Output panel's tab strip to minimise it to just its tabs; single-click a tab to restore it. The Explorer can sit on either side of the window — use View › Move Explorer Window Left/Right . View › Restore Main Window Size restores the main window to its default size. Note Layout is remembered between sessions: panel widths, the Output panel height, which panels were open, and the main window's position and maximised state are all stored in settings.ini . See Settings and configuration files . The title bar The window caption shows the active document and, when the workspace has been named, the project. An untitled workspace shows no project name — it is still a project, it just has not been saved under a name yet. See Projects and workspaces . Keyboard focus Focus moves with Tab between the editor and the panels, and clicking anywhere gives that region focus. If focus ever ends up somewhere unexpected, Ctrl + ` returns it to the editing window. Related topics Menu bar Side panels Output p"},{"u":"menu-bar.html","t":"Menu bar","s":"User Interface","k":"menu bar menus file edit search view project compile debug help accelerator shortcut compact menus","b":"How Tiko's menus are organised, how to read a menu item, and which menus rebuild themselves as you work. The menu bar carries every command in Tiko. It is drawn by the editor rather than by Windows, so it follows your chosen theme, but it behaves like a standard Windows menu: click to open, arrow keys to move, Enter to activate, Esc to close. The eight menus Menu Contents File New, Open, Open Recent, Close, Save, Save As, Rename, Duplicate, Settings, User Tools and Exit. Edit Undo and Redo, clipboard commands, line operations, commenting, selection and the Format submenu. Search Search Symbol, Find, Find Next/Previous, Find in Project, Replace, Goto Definition, navigation history, procedure navigation and bookmarks. View Panel visibility, zoom, editor splitting, code folding and window layout. Project New, Open, Recent Projects, Close, Save, Save As, Add Files and Project Options. Compile Build and Execute, Compile, Rebuild All, Compile Module, Quick Run, Run Executable and Command Line. Debug Start/Continue, Break, Stop, the stepping commands, Run to Cursor, breakpoints and Unused Symbols. Help Help Center and About. Reading a menu item Each item can carry three pieces of information: The command name — what it does. A name ending in an ellipsis ( … ) opens a dialog rather than acting immediately. The keyboard shortcut , shown right-aligned. This always reflects your current bindings, so if you remap a command the menu updates to match. A submenu arrow — Open Recent, Settings, User Tools, Format and Recent Projects each open a further menu. Note Items that cannot apply right now are shown dimmed. Most Debug commands, for example, are only available while a debugging session is running. Dynamic menus Four submenus are rebuilt each time you open them: File › Open Recent — the files you opened most recently. Project › Recent Projects — the workspaces you opened most recently. File › User Tools — the external tools you have defined. Debug — reflects whether a debugging session is active, showing Start Debugging or Continue Debugging . Both recent lists hold up to ten entries and end with Clear this list . An empty list shows a single dimmed (Empty) entry. Compact menus Menu rows can be drawn at a tighter height. Turn on Compact menus in File › Settings › Options… if you prefer denser menus or work on a small screen. Context menus always use the compact height —"},{"u":"toolbar.html","t":"Toolbar and icon strip","s":"User Interface","k":"toolbar icon strip buttons panel selector commands find save compile build debug","b":"Tiko's compact icon strip: which buttons switch panels, which run commands, and how to hide it. Tiko does not have a traditional wide toolbar across the top of the window. Instead a narrow icon strip runs down the side of the window, beside the side panel. It does the same job in less space. The strip is split into two groups: Panel selectors at one end — Explorer, Functions, Bookmarks and Settings. These latch : the active panel stays highlighted, and an underline marks which one you are looking at. Commands at the other end — Find, Save, Debug, Compile and Build. These are momentary buttons that fire and return to normal. Note Hover any button to see a tooltip naming the command and its keyboard shortcut. Buttons Button Action Shortcut Explorer Show the project Explorer in the side panel. Ctrl + F4 Functions Show the function list for the current file. F4 Bookmarks Show the bookmarks list. Shift + F4 Settings Open the options dialog. Ctrl + , Find Open the Find bar. Ctrl + F Save Save the current document. Ctrl + S Debug Start or continue debugging. F6 Compile Compile without running. Ctrl + F5 Build Build and execute. F5 TODO — verify icon strip contents Confirm the exact button set and their order against the shipping build, and add a close-up screenshot of the icon strip. Panel icon strip Close-up screenshot of the icon strip Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the icon strip showing both groups. Hiding the strip The icon strip is part of the side panel, so Ctrl + B hides and shows both together. With them hidden, the editor takes the whole window width. Related topics Side panels User tools — add your own external commands. Keyboard shortcuts"},{"u":"side-panels.html","t":"Side panels","s":"User Interface","k":"side panel explorer functions bookmarks dock tree navigation","b":"The Explorer, function list and bookmarks views that share Tiko's side dock, and how to switch, resize and hide them. The side panel is a single dock that shows one of three views at a time. Switch between them with the icon strip or their keyboard shortcuts. View Shows Shortcut Explorer The files in the current workspace, grouped by category. Ctrl + F4 Functions Every procedure in the current file or project. F4 Bookmarks Every bookmark you have set. Shift + F4 Ctrl + B shows and hides the whole panel, including the icon strip. The panel can be docked on either side of the window via View › Move Explorer Window Left/Right . Explorer The Explorer is a tree of the files in your workspace, grouped under five category headers. The headers are always present, whether or not they currently hold files. Click a file to open it, or to bring its tab forward if it is already open. Right-click for a context menu of file commands. Use the expand-all and collapse-all commands to open or close the whole tree at once. While the workspace is untitled, a pinned Save as Project… row sits at the top — the quickest way to give the workspace a name. Note Closing a file's tab does not remove it from the workspace. Files are removed explicitly. See Adding and removing project files . Explorer panel Screenshot of the Explorer tree with categories Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the Explorer showing the five category headers and several files. Functions The function list shows the procedures Tiko's parser found in your code — subs, functions, and their containing types. Click an entry to jump to its definition. The list follows the active document as you switch tabs. It refreshes as you type, because parsing runs in the background. Expand-all and collapse-all commands are available for the tree. Tip The function list is the fastest way to navigate a long file. For jumping across the whole project , use Search Symbol ( Ctrl + P ) instead. Bookmarks The bookmarks list gathers every bookmark in one place, showing the file and line of each. Click one to jump to it. Set and clear bookmarks with Ctrl + F2 , and cycle through them with F2 and Shift + F2 . Ctrl + Shift + F2 clears them all. Related topics Project Explorer — the Explorer in depth. Navigation Bookmarks"},{"u":"output-panel.html","t":"Output panel","s":"User Interface","k":"output panel compiler results search results todo notes errors warnings minimise dock","b":"The docked panel below the editor: compiler output, search results, collected TODO comments and project notes. The Output panel is docked below the editor and carries everything Tiko has to tell you, on separate tabs. Toggle it with Ctrl + F9 . Tab Contents Compiler Build output — the command line used, compiler messages, and the result. Errors and warnings are clickable. Search results Matches from Find in Project. Each row is a file, line and the matching text; click to jump there. TODO Every TODO comment found in the current file or project, collected automatically. Notes A free-text scratchpad saved with the workspace. Use it for anything you want to remember about this project. Working with results Click a row to open that file and jump to the line. Compiler errors carry the file, line and message exactly as the compiler reported them. The Search results and TODO lists are multi-column and sortable by clicking a column header. Minimising Double-click the tab strip to collapse the panel down to just its tabs, reclaiming the vertical space while leaving the tabs within reach. Click any tab to restore it. Drag the splitter above the panel to set a specific height. Note The panel's height, which tab was active, and whether it was open or minimised are all remembered between sessions. Notes The Notes tab is a plain multi-line text field stored with the workspace, not with any one file. It survives closing and reopening the project. Use it for build reminders, outstanding questions or a scratch list. Related topics Compiler errors and warnings Find in Project Building programs"},{"u":"tabs-and-splits.html","t":"Tabs and split views","s":"User Interface","k":"tabs document tabs close reorder tab list split view split editor left right top bottom","b":"Managing document tabs, and splitting the editor into two synchronised views of the same file. Every open document gets a tab above the editor. The tab strip scrolls when there are more tabs than fit. Working with tabs Action How Switch to a tab Click it, or use Ctrl + Tab and Ctrl + Shift + Tab . Close a tab Click its × button, or press Ctrl + W . Close every tab Ctrl + Shift + W Reorder tabs Drag a tab along the strip. Scroll the strip Use the scroll buttons at the end of the strip. List all open files Open the tab list button at the end of the strip — useful when more files are open than fit. Tab context menu Right-click a tab for close, save and file commands. Note A tab marks its document as modified until you save. Closing a modified document prompts you first. Split views The editor can be split into two views of the same document, so you can look at two parts of one file at once — a declaration at the top and its use hundreds of lines further down, for instance. Edits in either view appear immediately in the other. Command Shortcut Toggle split left/right Ctrl + \\ Toggle split top/bottom Ctrl + Shift + \\ Both commands toggle: run the same one again to return to a single view. Drag the splitter between the two views to change their relative sizes. Tip Split top/bottom suits reading code; split left/right suits comparing two procedures side by side on a wide screen. Related topics Navigation — moving between files and symbols. The main window"},{"u":"status-bar.html","t":"Status bar","s":"User Interface","k":"status bar line column caret position insert overwrite encoding line endings crlf language build configuration","b":"What each status bar field reports, and which ones you can click to change a document setting. The status bar along the bottom of the window reports the state of the active document. Several of its fields are interactive. Field Shows Click to Caret position Current line and column number. Nothing — informational. Insert mode Whether typing inserts or overwrites. Toggle, or press Insert . Encoding The document's character encoding, such as UTF-8. Change the encoding — see Encoding . Line endings CRLF (Windows), LF (Unix) or CR. Change the line-ending style. Language The syntax highlighting scheme in use. Choose a different language for this file. Build configuration The active build configuration. Switch configuration — see Build configurations . TODO — verify status bar fields Confirm the exact field set, order and click behaviour of the status bar against the shipping build, and add an annotated close-up screenshot. Status bar Annotated close-up of the status bar Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the status bar with each field labelled. Related topics Encoding and line endings Build configurations The main window"},{"u":"context-menus.html","t":"Context menus","s":"User Interface","k":"context menu right click popup menu editor tab explorer output","b":"The right-click menus available across the editor, panels, tabs and dialogs. Right-clicking almost anything in Tiko opens a context menu relevant to what is under the pointer. Context menus are drawn by the editor and follow your theme, and they always use the compact row height. Right-click on You get The editor surface Clipboard commands, selection, commenting, formatting, Goto Definition and bookmark commands. A document tab Close, close others, save, and file commands for that tab. The Explorer tree Open, remove from project, rename and file commands. The function list Jump to definition and list commands. The Output panel Copy, clear and result-list commands. A text field in a dialog Cut, copy, paste, select all and undo. Note Context menus support full keyboard navigation: arrow keys to move, Enter to choose, Esc to dismiss. They also close when you click outside them. TODO — enumerate context menu items Enumerate the exact items on each context menu from the shipping build and replace the summary table above with per-menu item lists. Related topics Menu reference Dialog reference"},{"u":"dialogs.html","t":"Dialog boxes","s":"User Interface","k":"dialog dialogs options themes build configurations keyboard user tools format options project options find replace about cancel ok","b":"How Tiko's dialogs behave, and an index of the main ones with what each is for. Tiko's dialogs are owner-drawn, so they follow your theme rather than the system grey of standard Windows dialogs. They behave conventionally in every other respect. Common behaviour Enter activates the default button; Esc cancels and closes without saving changes. Tab and Shift + Tab move between controls. The focused control is outlined. Cancel means cancel. Dialogs edit a working copy of your settings, so closing with Cancel leaves everything exactly as it was. Dialogs remember the page you last had open where that makes sense. Important Because settings dialogs work on a copy, nothing you change takes effect until you choose OK . Conversely, once you choose OK the change is written to settings.ini immediately. The main dialogs Dialog Opened from Purpose Options File › Settings › Options… Every editor behaviour, across eight pages. Themes File › Settings › Themes… Choose and edit colour themes. Build Configurations File › Settings › Build Configurations… Define named sets of compiler switches. Keyboard Shortcuts File › Settings › Keyboard Shortcuts… Remap any command. User Tools File › User Tools… Define external programs to run from the menu. Format Options Edit › Format › Format Options… Configure the code formatter, with a live preview. Project Options Project › Project Options… Per-project compiler options and build output settings. Find and Replace Ctrl + F / Ctrl + H Search within the current document. Find in Project Ctrl + Shift + F Search across every file in the workspace. About Help › About Version, credits and licence. Related topics Dialog reference — every dialog, control by control. Configuration reference"},{"u":"editing-basics.html","t":"Editing basics","s":"Editing Text","k":"edit caret cursor movement selection select all select line clipboard cut copy paste undo redo insert overwrite home end","b":"Caret movement, selecting text, the clipboard, undo and redo, and insert mode. Tiko's editing surface is built on Scintilla, so if you have used Notepad++, SciTE or any other Scintilla-based editor, the fundamentals will already feel familiar. Moving the caret Movement Keys One character / line ← → ↑ ↓ One word Ctrl + ← / Ctrl + → Start / end of line Home / End Start / end of document Ctrl + Home / Ctrl + End One screen PgUp / PgDn Next / previous procedure Ctrl + PgDn / Ctrl + PgUp Matching brace See Brace matching . Note Home is smart: it moves to the first non-blank character of the line, and pressing it again moves to column one. This is what you want almost every time on indented code. The Confine caret option keeps the caret inside the text rather than letting it move into the empty space past the end of a line. It is on by default. See Configuration reference . Selecting text With the keyboard Hold Shift and use any movement key. The selection extends from where the caret was to where it lands, so Ctrl + Shift + End selects to the end of the document. With the mouse Drag to select a range. Double-click selects a word. Triple-click selects a line. Click, then Shift-click elsewhere to select everything between. Click in the left margin to select that line. Selection commands Command Shortcut Notes Select All Ctrl + A The whole document. Select Line Ctrl + L The current line including its line ending. Repeat to extend downward. Clipboard Command Shortcut Behaviour Cut Ctrl + X With no selection, cuts the whole current line. Copy Ctrl + C With no selection, copies the whole current line. Paste Ctrl + V Replaces the selection if there is one, otherwise inserts at the caret. Delete Line Ctrl + Y Removes the line entirely. Tip Cut and Copy acting on the whole line when nothing is selected is a real time-saver: to move a line, put the caret on it, press Ctrl + X , move, and press Ctrl + V . Pasting into the editor pastes plain text. Rich formatting from a word processor or web page is discarded, so pasted code arrives as code. Undo and redo Ctrl + Z undoes and Ctrl + Shift + Z redoes, with no practical limit within an editing session. Undo history belongs to a document and is discarded when you close it. Important Some operations that rewrite an entire buffer — notably changing a document's encoding — can clear the undo history. Save before you change encodin"},{"u":"multiple-selections.html","t":"Multiple selections and column mode","s":"Editing Text","k":"multiple selections multi cursor multi caret column mode rectangular selection alt drag vertical edit","b":"Editing in several places at once: rectangular column selections and independent multiple carets. Tiko supports editing in more than one place at once. There are two related mechanisms, and knowing which one you want saves a lot of fiddling. Column (rectangular) selection A column selection covers a rectangle of characters rather than a run of text. It is the right tool when the thing you want to change lines up vertically. Method How Mouse Hold Alt and drag. Keyboard Hold Alt + Shift and use the arrow keys. With a column selected, typing replaces the rectangle on every line at once, and Delete deletes it. A zero-width column selection — one made without moving horizontally — gives you a caret on each line, so typing inserts the same text into every one. fb Column editing Copy ' Before: select the zero-width column at the start of these lines Dim As Integer width Dim As Integer height Dim As Integer depth ' Then type \"Static \" once: Static Dim As Integer width Static Dim As Integer height Static Dim As Integer depth Multiple selections Multiple selections are independent — they need not line up. Hold Ctrl while dragging or clicking to add another selection or caret. Typing then affects all of them. Each selection keeps its own caret, so word movement and %s behave sensibly in each. Click anywhere without %s to collapse back to a single caret. Additional carets and selections are drawn using dedicated theme colours, so they stay visible against your chosen background. See The theme editor . Tip Multiple selections shine for renaming a local variable within one procedure: select each occurrence with Ctrl -drag and type the new name once. For a project-wide rename, use Replace instead — it is safer and reviewable. Which should I use? Task Use Add a prefix or suffix to a run of consecutive lines Column selection Delete a vertical strip, such as an old indent level Column selection Edit occurrences scattered around the file Multiple selections Change the same word everywhere in a file or project Find and Replace Related topics Editing basics Find and Replace Line and block operations"},{"u":"indentation.html","t":"Indentation","s":"Editing Text","k":"indent indentation tabs spaces tab size auto indent smart indent block indent unindent indent guides for next variable auto complete","b":"Tabs versus spaces, tab size, block indenting, automatic indentation and block completion, and indent guides. Indentation settings are per-installation rather than per-file, and live in File › Settings › Options… . Tabs versus spaces The Tab indents with spaces setting decides what the Tab key actually inserts: Setting Pressing Tab inserts Use when On (default) Spaces, up to the next tab stop. You want the file to look identical everywhere, regardless of the reader's tab width. Off A literal tab character. Your project's convention is tabs, or you are matching an existing file. Tab size sets how many columns a tab stop occupies. The default is 4. Important Changing these settings affects what you type from now on. It does not convert what is already in the file — use the formatter for that. Indenting blocks Command Shortcut Behaviour Indent block Tab With a multi-line selection, indents every selected line by one level. Unindent block Shift + Tab Removes one level of indent from every selected line. Note With no selection, Tab inserts an indent at the caret as usual. The block behaviour only applies when lines are selected. Automatic indentation With Auto indentation on — the default — pressing Enter carries the current line's indent forward to the new line, so you stay in position while typing nested code. Tiko goes further for FreeBASIC. When you press Enter after a line that opens a block, it can insert the matching closing statement for you and place the caret between them: fb Automatic block completion Copy ' Type this and press Enter at the end of the line: If count > 0 Then ' Tiko produces: If count > 0 Then | <- caret lands here End If This applies to If , For , Do , While , Select Case , Sub , Function , Type , Scope and the other block constructs. Tiko scans forward from the line you are on to check whether the block is already closed, and inserts nothing if it is. Two related options control the detail: Auto complete — turn this off and Enter carries the indent forward but inserts no closing statement. For/Next variable — when a For block is completed, include the loop variable on the Next line. Indent guides Indent guides are faint vertical lines showing each indent level, which makes deeply nested code much easier to read. Turn them on in the options dialog; their colour comes from the current theme. Converting existing indentation To change the"},{"u":"encoding.html","t":"Encoding and line endings","s":"Editing Text","k":"encoding utf-8 utf8 unicode ansi bom byte order mark codepage line endings crlf lf cr newline convert","b":"Character encodings, UTF-8 and Unicode, byte-order marks, and the three line-ending conventions — how to see and change each. Every text file is a sequence of bytes, and an encoding is the rule for turning those bytes into characters. Choose the wrong one and accented letters, symbols and non-Latin scripts come out as garbage. Encodings Tiko works with Encoding Notes UTF-8 The modern default. Represents every Unicode character, and is byte-compatible with ASCII for plain English text. Use this unless you have a reason not to. UTF-8 with BOM UTF-8 preceded by a byte-order mark. Some tools require it; others choke on it. ANSI / system code page The legacy Windows encoding for your locale. Fine for plain ASCII, unreliable for anything else. UTF-16 Two bytes per character. Common in Windows APIs, less so in source files. TODO — verify supported encodings Confirm the exact set of encodings Tiko offers, and the precise wording of the encoding options, against the shipping build. Seeing and changing the encoding The status bar shows the current document's encoding. Click it to convert the document to a different encoding. Warning Converting to a narrower encoding can lose characters permanently. Converting a UTF-8 file containing, say, Greek text to ANSI will destroy that text. Save a copy first if you are unsure. Two settings govern the defaults: New file encoding The encoding given to files you create. Unicode encoding How Tiko treats files whose encoding it cannot determine. Line endings Text files mark the end of a line in one of three ways: Style Bytes Convention CRLF 0D 0A Windows. The default for new files. LF 0A Unix, Linux, macOS. Common in cross-platform projects and in Git repositories. CR 0D Classic Mac OS. Rare today. The status bar shows the current style; click it to convert the whole document. Tiko detects the style when it opens a file and preserves it on save, so opening a Unix-style file and saving it does not silently rewrite every line. Tip Mixed line endings in one file cause odd-looking diffs and confuse some compilers. If a file behaves strangely, converting it to a single consistent style is a cheap fix. Unicode in source code FreeBASIC source is normally plain ASCII, but string literals and comments may contain anything. If your program handles non-English text, save the source as UTF-8 and be explicit in code about how those strings are e"},{"u":"view-options.html","t":"Display and view options","s":"Editing Text","k":"view display margins line numbers fold margin right edge highlight current line indent guides whitespace word wrap zoom scroll minimap occurrence","b":"Margins, line numbers, indent guides, the right-edge marker, whitespace display, word wrap, zoom and scrolling. These options change how your code is displayed without changing a byte of it. Most live in File › Settings › Options… ; the zoom commands are on the View menu. Margins Margin Shows Setting Line numbers The number of each line. Line numbering Left margin A narrow gutter for bookmarks and breakpoint markers. Left margin Fold margin Fold markers beside collapsible blocks. Fold margin Click the left margin beside a line to select it. If Click toggles breakpoint is on, clicking the margin sets or clears a breakpoint instead — convenient while debugging. See Breakpoints . Visual guides Highlight current line Tints the line the caret is on, so you never lose it in a large file. Right edge Draws a vertical line at a chosen column — 80 by default — as a reminder of your preferred maximum line length. Turn it on with Right edge and set the column with Right edge position . Indent guides Faint vertical lines marking each level of indentation. Brace highlighting Highlights the brace or bracket matching the one beside the caret, and marks unmatched ones. See Brace matching . Occurrence highlighting Highlights every other occurrence of the word under the caret. Whitespace and word wrap Displaying whitespace draws dots for spaces and arrows for tabs, which is the quickest way to diagnose a file with mixed indentation. Word wrap folds long lines to the window width instead of scrolling horizontally. TODO — verify whitespace and word wrap commands Confirm where the whitespace-display and word-wrap toggles live in the shipping build's options dialog or View menu, and document the exact commands. Zoom Command Shortcut Zoom In Ctrl + + Zoom Out Ctrl + - Zoom Reset Ctrl + 0 Zoom is a temporary display change — it does not alter the configured font size, and Ctrl + 0 returns to it. Ctrl and the scroll wheel zoom too. Tip Zoom out to get an overview of a file's shape when you are looking for a particular block, then reset. It is faster than scrolling. Scrolling The mouse wheel scrolls vertically; Shift and the wheel scrolls horizontally. The Position middle option keeps the caret line vertically centred when you jump to a line, rather than leaving it at the very top or bottom of the view. Both scrollbars are drawn by the editor and follow your theme. Minimap TODO — conf"},{"u":"code-folding.html","t":"Code folding","s":"Editing Text","k":"fold folding collapse expand outline fold all unfold all fold margin fold point","b":"Collapsing and expanding blocks of code to hide detail, with the mouse or the keyboard. Folding collapses a block of code to a single line so you can hide detail you are not working on. Nothing is removed — folding is purely a display state. Folding with the mouse With the fold margin enabled, a marker appears beside every foldable block. Click it to collapse or expand that block. A collapsed block shows a marker you can click again to reopen it. Folding commands Command Shortcut What it does Toggle Current Fold Point F8 Collapses or expands the block containing the caret. Toggle Current And All Below Ctrl + F8 Folds the current block and every block nested inside it. Fold All Shift + F8 Collapses every block in the document — an instant outline of the file. Unfold All Ctrl + Shift + F8 Expands everything. Tip Shift + F8 followed by expanding just the procedure you want is a fast way to orient yourself in an unfamiliar file. The F4 function list does the same job without changing the view. Note Which constructs are foldable comes from the syntax lexer for the file's language. For FreeBASIC that means procedures, types, and the usual block statements. Fold state is a view property, not part of the file. Searching finds text inside folded blocks, and jumping to a line inside one expands it automatically. Related topics Display and view options Navigation"},{"u":"bookmarks.html","t":"Bookmarks","s":"Editing Text","k":"bookmark bookmarks toggle next previous clear list marker margin todo","b":"Marking lines to return to, cycling through them, and the bookmarks list panel. A bookmark marks a line you want to come back to. Bookmarks are ideal while working across several places in a large file — set one at each, then cycle between them. Commands Command Shortcut What it does Toggle Bookmark Ctrl + F2 Sets a bookmark on the current line, or clears the one already there. Next Bookmark F2 Jumps to the next bookmark, wrapping at the end. Previous Bookmark Shift + F2 Jumps to the previous one. Clear Bookmarks Ctrl + Shift + F2 Removes them all. View Bookmarks List Shift + F4 Shows every bookmark in the side panel. A bookmarked line is marked in the left margin. The bookmarks list The bookmarks panel lists every bookmark with its file and line. Click an entry to jump there. Because it spans files, it doubles as a lightweight to-do list for a task that touches several places in the project. Bookmarks and TODO comments Bookmarks and TODO comments solve related problems in different ways: Bookmarks TODO comments Stored In the editor's session state. In the source file itself. Visible to others No. Yes — anyone reading the code sees them. Where listed Bookmarks panel. Output panel, TODO tab. Best for Navigating while you work. Recording work that still needs doing. Tip Use bookmarks for the next ten minutes and TODO comments for next week. Related topics Navigation Side panels Output panel — the TODO list."},{"u":"find-replace.html","t":"Find and Replace","s":"Searching","k":"find search replace replace all find next find previous f3 incremental match case whole word wrap search up history","b":"Searching within the current document, the search options, replacing matches, and search history. Find and Replace work within the current document . To search every file in the workspace, use Find in Project instead. Find Press Ctrl + F , or choose Search › Find… . Type what you are looking for. If text was selected when you opened Find, it is used as the initial search term. Press Enter to go to the next match, Shift + Enter for the previous one. Press Esc to close the Find bar. The search term is remembered. Important F3 and Shift + F3 repeat the last search from anywhere in the editor — you do not need the Find bar open. This is the fastest way to step through matches while editing. Search options Option Effect Match case Print no longer matches print . Off by default, which suits FreeBASIC's case-insensitive keywords. Whole word Matches only complete words: count will not match counter or rowcount . Regular expression Treats the search text as a pattern rather than literal text. See Regular expressions . Search up Searches backwards from the caret rather than forwards. Wrap around Continues from the top when the search reaches the end of the document. TODO — verify Find bar options Confirm the exact option set and labels on the Find and Replace bars against the shipping build, and replace the placeholder below with a real screenshot. Find bar Screenshot of the Find bar with its options Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the Find bar showing the search field and option toggles. Replace Press Ctrl + H , or choose Search › Replace… . Button What it does Find Next Moves to the next match without changing anything. Replace Replaces the current match, then finds the next one. This is the safe, reviewable option. Replace All Replaces every match in the document in one step, as a single undoable action. Warning Replace All is a single Ctrl + Z away if it goes wrong — but check your options first. A Replace All with Whole word off is the classic way to turn every id into identifier inside longer words. Tip Before a large Replace All, run the same search with Ctrl + Shift + F to see how many places it will touch and whether they are all what you expect. Incremental searching The Find bar highlights matches as you type, so you can see whether a search term is going to be too broad before you commit to it. Refine the term and the highlighting updates. Search hi"},{"u":"find-in-project.html","t":"Find in Project","s":"Searching","k":"find in project search project search all files grep results output panel ctrl shift f","b":"Searching every file in the workspace at once, and working through the results in the Output panel. Find in Project searches every file in the current workspace and collects the results in the Output panel, where each match is a clickable link to its line. Press Ctrl + Shift + F , or choose Search › Find in Project… . Enter the search text and set any options you need. Start the search. The Output panel switches to its Search results tab. Click any result to open that file and jump to the line. Options Find in Project offers the same matching options as Find — case sensitivity, whole word and regular expressions — plus filters over which files are searched. TODO — verify Find in Project filters Confirm the exact filter controls (file masks, folder scope, include/exclude patterns) offered by the Find in Project dialog in the shipping build, and document each one. Find in Project Screenshot of the Find in Project dialog Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the dialog showing its options and filter fields. Reading the results Each result row shows the file, the line number and the matching line's text. The list is multi-column — click a header to sort by that column, which is a quick way to group every hit in one file together. Note Results stay in the panel until your next project search, so you can work through a long list at your own pace. Switching tabs in the Output panel does not clear them. Typical uses Before renaming anything. Search for the identifier first to see every place it appears, including comments and strings that a rename would miss. Finding where a procedure is called from. Goto Definition ( F12 ) goes the other way; this finds the callers. Auditing. Search for a pattern such as TODO , HACK or a deprecated API name across the whole codebase. Tip Tiko already collects TODO comments automatically into the Output panel's TODO tab — you do not need to search for those. Related topics Find and Replace Regular expressions Output panel Projects and workspaces — what \"the project\" means for a search."},{"u":"regular-expressions.html","t":"Regular expressions","s":"Searching","k":"regular expression regex pattern search wildcard capture group replace backreference anchor character class","b":"Pattern-based searching: the common syntax, using captured groups in replacements, and practical patterns for FreeBASIC source. Turning on the Regular expression option changes the search text from something matched literally into a pattern . Patterns let you find things you could not otherwise describe — any number, any line starting with a comment, an identifier followed by an opening parenthesis. Warning Regular-expression syntax varies between tools. The table below covers constructs that are near-universal and safe to rely on. Confirm the exact dialect against the shipping build before depending on anything more exotic. TODO — confirm the regular expression dialect Confirm which regular-expression engine Tiko uses for Find and for Find in Project (the Scintilla built-in engine and its C++ std::regex option behave differently), and document the supported syntax precisely. Pattern basics Pattern Matches Example . Any single character b.t matches bat , bit , but [abc] Any one of the listed characters [0-9] matches any digit [^abc] Any character not listed * Zero or more of the preceding item ab*c matches ac , abc , abbc + One or more of the preceding item ? Zero or one of the preceding item ^ Start of a line ^Dim matches Dim only at the line start $ End of a line \\d A digit \\w A word character (letter, digit or underscore) \\s Whitespace \\ Escapes the next character \\. matches a literal full stop (…) A group, captured for use in the replacement | Either alternative Sub|Function matches either word Using captures in a replacement Text captured by a group in the search pattern can be inserted into the replacement, normally with \\1 for the first group, \\2 for the second and so on. This is what makes regular-expression replace powerful rather than merely clever. text A capture-and-reuse replacement Copy Find: Print (.+) Replace: Debug.Write \\1 ' Before Print \"starting\" Print count ' After Debug.Write \"starting\" Debug.Write count Practical FreeBASIC patterns Goal Pattern Lines that are only a comment ^\\s*' Any Sub or Function declaration ^\\s*(Sub|Function)\\s+\\w+ A number literal \\b\\d+\\b Trailing whitespace \\s+$ An empty line ^$ A call to a specific procedure \\bDrawBox\\s*\\( Tip Test a pattern with Find first, then switch to Replace once you are satisfied it matches exactly what you intended. A pattern that is slightly too greedy can rewrite far more than you expe"},{"u":"navigation.html","t":"Navigation","s":"Navigation","k":"navigation goto definition f12 go back go forward search symbol ctrl p goto line next function previous function header file main file resource file switch tabs","b":"Goto Definition, navigation history, symbol search, procedure stepping, and jumping between related and open files. Tiko gives you several ways to move around, from the whole-project symbol search down to stepping between procedures in one file. Learning two or three of these makes far more difference to day-to-day speed than any other feature. Goto Definition Put the caret on any symbol — a procedure name, a type, a variable — and press F12 . Tiko jumps to where that symbol is defined, opening the file if necessary. This works across the whole project because Tiko's parser has already indexed every file. It is the single most useful navigation command in the editor. Note For a procedure with a separate declaration and implementation, Goto Definition takes you to the implementation — the code, rather than the prototype. Navigation history Every jump is recorded, so you can retrace your steps the way you would in a browser. Command Shortcut Go Back Alt + ← Go Forward Alt + → Tip F12 then Alt + ← is the core navigation loop: dive into a definition, read it, and come straight back to where you were. Learn this pair first. Search Symbol Ctrl + P opens Search Symbol — a filter box over every symbol in the project. Start typing a procedure or type name and the list narrows as you type; choose an entry to jump straight to it. Use this when you know what you are looking for but not which file it is in. It is the closest thing Tiko has to a universal \"go to anything\" command. TODO — verify Search Symbol matching Confirm whether Search Symbol also matches file names and line numbers, or symbols only, and document its matching rules (substring, prefix or fuzzy). Moving between procedures Command Shortcut Next procedure Ctrl + PgDn Previous procedure Ctrl + PgUp These step through the subs and functions of the current file in order — a quick way to skim a file's structure without leaving the keyboard. Goto Line Compiler messages and colleagues both talk in line numbers. To jump to a specific line, use the Goto command and enter the number. TODO — verify the Goto Line command Confirm the exact Goto Line command and its default shortcut in the shipping build and document it here. Note Clicking a compiler error in the Output panel jumps to the right line directly, so you rarely need to type a line number by hand. Switching between related files Tiko knows about the convent"},{"u":"symbol-navigation.html","t":"Symbols and the function list","s":"Navigation","k":"symbol symbols parser function list outline procedures types enums variables unused symbols code tips index","b":"How Tiko's background parser indexes your code, and the features that read from it: the function list, code tips and the unused-symbols report. Tiko continuously parses your source in the background and keeps a database of the symbols it finds — procedures and their parameters, types, enumerations, variables — each with the file, line and column where it was declared. Several features read from that one database. What the parser extracts Symbol kind Recorded Subs and functions Name, parameters, return type, and where defined. Types and unions Name, members, and where defined. Enumerations Name and members. Variables Name, type, and scope — including procedure locals. Note Parsing runs on a worker thread, so it never blocks typing. Results refresh as you edit — you do not need to save a file for its symbols to be picked up. The function list F4 shows every procedure in the current file in the side panel. Click one to jump to it. It is the fastest way to move around a long file, and it doubles as an outline of the file's structure. The list follows the active document as you switch tabs, and refreshes as you type. Expand-all and collapse-all commands are available for the tree. Unused symbols Debug › Unused Symbols… reports symbols the parser found declared but could not find used. It is a useful periodic tidy-up: dead procedures, leftover variables and stale types. Warning Treat the report as a list of candidates, not a list of certainties. A symbol reached only through a function pointer, a conditional-compilation branch or an external reference can look unused while being essential. Code tips When you type an opening parenthesis after a procedure name, Tiko shows a code tip — a small popup giving that procedure's parameter list. It closes when you type the closing parenthesis, press Esc , or move the caret off the line. Code tips read the same symbol database, so they work for your own procedures as well as library ones. Turn them off with the Code tips option. Related topics Navigation Autocomplete and code tips Side panels"},{"u":"autocomplete.html","t":"Autocomplete and code tips","s":"Editing Productivity","k":"autocomplete auto complete completion intellisense code tips calltip parameter hints character auto completion keywords","b":"The completion list and parameter hints: how to use them, what they offer, and the settings that control them. Autocomplete offers completions as you type, drawn from the symbols Tiko has parsed out of your project plus the keyword lists for the current language. Using autocomplete Start typing an identifier. Once there is enough to narrow the field, a list appears beneath the caret. Keep typing to filter it further — the list narrows with every character. Move through the list with ↑ and ↓ . Press Enter or Tab to accept the highlighted entry. Press Esc to dismiss the list and carry on typing. Note Typing ( also accepts the highlighted entry when it is a procedure that takes parameters — and, because the parenthesis is inserted too, the code tip showing the parameter list appears immediately. What is offered Procedures, types, enumerations and variables from your project. Language keywords from the keyword lists in settings\\keywords . Entries are sorted alphabetically. The keyword lists are editable — see Syntax highlighting . The same lists drive highlighting and autocomplete, so a keyword you add is offered as well as coloured. Settings Auto complete Turns the completion list on and off. Code tips Turns parameter hints on and off. Character auto completion Automatically inserts the closing member of a pair when you type an opening quotation mark, parenthesis or bracket. Code tips A code tip appears when you open a parenthesis after a known procedure name and shows its parameters. It follows the caret as you type arguments and disappears when the call is complete. A code tip closes when you: type the closing parenthesis; press Esc ; move the caret off the line, or back before the opening parenthesis; switch to another document. Tip If a tip does not appear for one of your own procedures, the parser may not have seen the file yet — it is indexed as part of the project, so check the file is actually in the workspace. See Project files . Related topics Symbols and the function list Syntax highlighting Configuration reference"},{"u":"line-operations.html","t":"Line and block operations","s":"Editing Productivity","k":"duplicate line delete line move line up down comment block uncomment select line uppercase lowercase mixed case sort join transpose trim","b":"Duplicating, deleting, moving and selecting lines; commenting blocks; and converting the case of a selection. These commands act on whole lines or blocks, and save a great deal of selecting and dragging. Most work with no selection at all, acting on the current line. Line commands Command Shortcut What it does Duplicate Line Ctrl + D Copies the current line and inserts the copy below it. Delete Line Ctrl + Y Removes the current line entirely. Move Line Up Alt + ↑ Swaps the current line with the one above. Moves the whole selection if there is one. Move Line Down Alt + ↓ Swaps it with the line below. Select Line Ctrl + L Selects the whole current line. New line below Ctrl + Enter Opens a line beneath the current one from anywhere on it. Tip Alt + ↑ is the fastest way to reorder statements or move a procedure — select the lines and hold the shortcut down. Commenting Command Shortcut What it does Comment Block Ctrl + / Comments out every selected line, or the current line. UnComment Block Ctrl + Shift + / Removes the comment marks again. The comment character used matches the current file's language — an apostrophe for FreeBASIC, // for C-family files. Changing case Command Shortcut Result Uppercase Ctrl + Alt + U COUNT Lowercase Ctrl + Alt + L count Mixed case Ctrl + Alt + X Count These act on the selection. With nothing selected, there is nothing to convert. Note To normalise the case of keywords throughout a file, use the formatter's case rules instead — see Code formatting . These commands change whatever is selected, keyword or not. Indenting blocks Tab and Shift + Tab indent and unindent every line of a multi-line selection. See Indentation . Sorting, joining and trimming lines TODO — confirm sort / join / transpose / trim commands Confirm whether the shipping build provides Sort Lines, Join Lines, Transpose Lines and a standalone Trim Trailing Whitespace command, and document them here. The formatter provides trailing-whitespace trimming as a rule; a separate editor command may or may not exist. The formatter's Trim trailing whitespace rule removes trailing spaces across a document — see Code formatting . Related topics Editing basics Code formatting Keyboard shortcuts"},{"u":"syntax-highlighting.html","t":"Syntax highlighting","s":"Editing Productivity","k":"syntax highlighting colours lexer lexilla keywords keyword lists language keyword case proper case","b":"How Tiko colours code, choosing the language for a file, editing the keyword lists, and the difference between displaying and rewriting keyword case. Syntax highlighting colours your code according to what each piece of it means — keywords, strings, comments, numbers, types. Tiko highlights through Lexilla, the lexer library that accompanies Scintilla. Turning it on and off The Syntax highlighting option controls it globally. It is on by default; turning it off displays everything in one colour, which can be marginally faster on extremely large files. Choosing the language for a file Tiko picks a language from the file's extension. The status bar shows the current choice and lets you override it for that document — useful for a file with an unusual extension, or a fragment you want highlighted as something else. Keyword lists The words highlighted as keywords come from plain text files in settings\\keywords . These are editable, and this is how you teach Tiko about an API it does not know about — a third-party library's function names, for example. Those same lists do three jobs at once: they decide what gets highlighted as a keyword; they supply entries to the autocomplete list; they provide the canonical spelling used by the formatter's keyword-case rules and by the editor's proper-case display. Important Because the canonical spelling comes from these files, enter each keyword the way you want to see it written — ByVal , ScreenRes , MessageBox — rather than in lower case. Matching itself is not case-sensitive, so the spelling in the file is purely about presentation. The keyword pages in the options dialog let you edit these lists without leaving the editor. TODO — enumerate keyword lists List the exact keyword list files shipped in settings\\keywords and what each one covers, and document the keyword editor pages in the options dialog. Keyword case display The Keyword case setting controls how keywords are displayed — unchanged, upper case, lower case or proper case. It is a display setting only: it never changes a byte in your file. Important This is a different thing from the formatter's Case keywords rule, which rewrites the file. The two can legitimately disagree — you might display keywords in proper case while leaving the file exactly as written. Colours Every syntactic element gets its colour from the current theme. To change one, use the theme edit"},{"u":"brace-matching.html","t":"Brace matching and occurrence highlighting","s":"Editing Productivity","k":"brace matching bracket parenthesis highlight unmatched occurrence highlighting word under caret","b":"Matching parentheses and brackets, spotting unbalanced ones, and highlighting every occurrence of the word under the caret. With Brace highlighting on, placing the caret beside a parenthesis, bracket or brace highlights it and its partner. If the partner is missing, the unmatched one is marked in a different colour. This is the quickest way to find an unbalanced expression — put the caret on the opening parenthesis and see where the highlight lands. Note Highlighting is off by default. Turn it on in File › Settings › Options… . Occurrence highlighting The related Occurrence highlighting option highlights every other appearance of the word under the caret. Put the caret on a variable and every use of it in view is marked, which makes tracing a value through a procedure much easier. Tip Occurrence highlighting is a fast, local alternative to running a search — but it is a visual aid only. Before you rename anything, use Find in Project to see every occurrence including the ones off screen. Related topics Display and view options Code folding Find in Project"},{"u":"code-formatting.html","t":"Code formatting","s":"Editing Productivity","k":"format formatter prettify beautify reindent format document format selection format project keyword case spacing blank lines trim trailing whitespace format on enter format on paste","b":"The configurable code formatter: the four commands, the rules, the guarantees it makes about your source, and formatting automatically on Enter or paste. The code formatter rewrites your source to a consistent style: indentation, keyword case, spacing around operators and commas, blank lines, trailing whitespace. Every rule can be turned on or off individually. The four commands Command Shortcut Scope Format Document Shift + Alt + F The whole of the active file. Format Selection — Only the selected lines. Format All Open Documents — Every document currently open. Format Project — Every file in the workspace. All four live on Edit › Format . What the formatter will never do Important The formatter never moves a line break. It will not split If x Then y into a block, will not join lines, and will not break a long line. Where your statements begin and end is your decision, not the formatter's. The only exception is the blank-line rules, which can add or remove empty lines. This boundary is what makes the formatter safe to run on a file you did not write. How it protects your code Every format runs two checks before anything reaches your buffer: Token equivalence. The formatted text is broken back into tokens and compared with the original. If a single non-whitespace token differs, the format is rejected. Stability. The result is formatted a second time. If the second pass differs from the first, the format is rejected. If either check fails, your original text is left exactly as it was. A formatter that cannot prove it preserved your code does not touch it. The rules Configure these in Edit › Format › Format Options… , which shows a live preview using the real formatting engine, so you can see the effect of a rule before committing to it. Rule Effect Reindent Recompute the indentation of every line from block structure. Indent Case Indent Case labels inside a Select Case block. Continuation indent How far to indent a continued line. Case keywords Rewrite keywords to their canonical spelling from the keyword lists. Case directives Rewrite preprocessor directives to canonical case. Case types Rewrite type names to canonical case. Space operators Ensure a space either side of binary operators. Space after comma Ensure a space after each comma. Parenthesis spacing Control spacing just inside parentheses. Spaces before comment Minimum spacing before a trailing comme"},{"u":"projects-overview.html","t":"Projects and workspaces","s":"Projects","k":"project workspace untitled named default.tiko save as project new project open project close project recent projects categories main module","b":"Tiko's single workspace model: why every set of open files is already a project, how to name one, and what a project contains. Important In Tiko, every workspace is a project. There is no \"no project open\" state. If you simply open a few loose files, those files are a project — an untitled one. Naming it later is a save, not a conversion. This one rule removes a great deal of the ceremony other editors ask for. You never have to create a project before you can work, and you never lose your work because you forgot to. Named and untitled workspaces Untitled workspace Named project Created by Just opening or creating files. Choosing Save as Project… and giving it a name. Stored in settings\\default.tiko , automatically. A .tiko file wherever you put it. Reopens on start-up Yes. Yes. Appears in Recent Projects No — it has no name. Yes. Shown in the window caption No project name. The project name. Has project options Yes. Yes. Because the untitled workspace is saved automatically, the files you had open are always restored the next time you start — whether or not you ever named the project. Creating a project Promote the workspace you already have This is the usual route, and the easiest: Open the files you want to work on. Click the pinned Save as Project… row at the top of the Explorer, or choose Project › Save Project As… . Choose a folder and a name. The workspace becomes a named project containing exactly the files you already had. Start from nothing Project › New Project… closes the current workspace and starts a fresh untitled one. There is no dialog — you are simply given a clean slate. Opening a project Project › Open Project… and choose a .tiko file. Project › Recent Projects lists the ten most recent, and ends with Clear this list . Tiko reopens your last workspace automatically when it starts. Note Opening a project closes the current workspace first, prompting you to save anything modified. If you cancel that prompt, the open is abandoned and nothing changes. Saving and closing Command What it does Save Project Writes the project file — its file list and options. Save Project As… Writes it under a new name; that becomes the current project. Also how an untitled workspace gets a name. Close Project Closes the workspace and every file in it, then starts a fresh untitled workspace — because there is always a workspace. Saving the project and saving your"},{"u":"project-explorer.html","t":"Project Explorer","s":"Projects","k":"explorer project explorer tree categories side panel open file context menu expand collapse","b":"The workspace tree: reading its categories, opening files from it, and the commands available on its context menu. The Explorer is the side panel view that shows your workspace as a tree. Open it with Ctrl + F4 or the Explorer button on the icon strip. Reading the tree Files are grouped under the five category headers described in Projects and workspaces . Every header is always shown, so an empty category is visible as an empty group rather than absent — which makes it obvious where a file would go. While the workspace is untitled, a pinned Save as Project… row sits at the top of the tree. It disappears once the project has a name. Project Explorer Screenshot of the Explorer tree Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the Explorer showing the category headers, several files, and the pinned Save as Project row. Working in the Explorer Action How Open a file Click it. If it is already open, its tab comes forward. Expand or collapse a group Click the twisty beside it. Expand or collapse everything Use the expand-all and collapse-all commands. File commands Right-click for the context menu. Move the panel to the other side View › Move Explorer Window Left/Right Hide the panel Ctrl + B Note Closing a file's tab does not remove it from the project. The tab and the project membership are separate things — closing the tab just stops displaying the file. It is still listed in the Explorer, still searched by Find in Project, and still compiled. Related topics Adding and removing files Side panels Projects and workspaces"},{"u":"project-files.html","t":"Adding and removing files","s":"Projects","k":"add files remove file project files rename duplicate template insert file main module resource file category","b":"Getting files into and out of a project, renaming and duplicating them, templates, and designating the main module. Adding files Route Use when Project › Add Files to Project… ( Ctrl + F11 ) Adding existing files. You can select several at once. File › New ( Ctrl + N ) Creating a new file. It joins the workspace when you save it. File › Open… ( Ctrl + O ) Opening an existing file. It joins the current workspace. Tiko categorises each added file by its extension. A .bas file entering a workspace that has no main module yet becomes the main module. Removing files Removing a file from a project takes it out of the project's file list. It does not delete the file from disk. Warning Do not confuse closing with removing. Ctrl + W closes a tab and leaves the file in the project; removing it from the Explorer takes it out of the project but leaves the file on disk. TODO — verify the remove-file command Confirm the exact Explorer context-menu command used to remove a file from a project, and whether Tiko offers to delete the file from disk as well. File commands Command Menu What it does Rename… File › Rename… Renames the file on disk and updates the project to match. Duplicate File › Duplicate Makes a copy of the file and adds it to the project. Open File As Template File › Open File As Template… Opens a file's contents as a new untitled document, leaving the original untouched — a starting point for a new file. Insert File Ctrl + I Inserts another file's contents into the current document at the caret. Tip Open File As Template is the clean way to start from boilerplate. Keep a skeleton .bas somewhere, open it as a template, and save the result under the new name — the skeleton itself is never at risk. The main module and the resource file A project designates one file as its main module — the one with the program entry point, which the build commands compile. One file can also be marked as the resource script . Jump to either at any time with Ctrl + Shift + M and Ctrl + Shift + R . TODO — verify how to set the main module Document the exact procedure for changing which file is the main module or the resource file in the shipping build. Related topics Project Explorer Project options Building programs"},{"u":"project-options.html","t":"Project options","s":"Projects","k":"project options command line arguments compiler options build output per project settings tiko file","b":"Per-project settings: command-line arguments, extra compiler switches and build output — and how they differ from build configurations and editor settings. Project options are settings that belong to this project , stored in its .tiko file rather than in your editor settings. Open them with Project › Project Options… . The pages Project Identifies the project and holds its command-line arguments — the parameters passed to your program when Tiko runs it. Set them here rather than remembering to type them each time. Compiler options Extra compiler switches for this project, added to whatever the active build configuration supplies. Use this for switches that are inherent to the project — an include path, a library it links against — rather than switches that vary between debug and release, which belong in a build configuration . Build output Where the compiler should put the executable it produces, and related output settings. TODO — enumerate Project Options fields Confirm the exact fields on each page of the Project Options dialog in the shipping build and replace the summaries above with a field-by-field table. Project Options Screenshot of the Project Options dialog Placeholder. Replace with a capture showing the three sections and their fields. Project options versus build configurations versus editor settings Setting lives in Scope Examples Editor settings ( settings.ini ) Your whole installation Font, theme, indentation, autocomplete, compiler path. Build configuration Reusable across projects Debug versus release switches, optimisation level, target architecture. Project options ( .tiko ) This project only Include paths for this project, its libraries, its command-line arguments, its output folder. Tip If you find yourself setting the same option in every project, it probably belongs in a build configuration instead. Related topics Build configurations Compiler setup Configuration reference"},{"u":"compiler-setup.html","t":"Compiler setup","s":"Building Programs","k":"compiler setup fbc path freebasic compiler configuration 32-bit 64-bit switches command line","b":"Pointing Tiko at your FreeBASIC compiler, choosing between 32-bit and 64-bit, and where compiler switches come from. Tiko does not include a compiler. It drives the FreeBASIC compiler you already have installed, so the first thing to do is tell it where that compiler is. Setting the compiler path Open File › Settings › Options… . Select the Compiler page. Browse to your FreeBASIC installation and select the compiler executable — fbc.exe , or fbc64.exe / fbc32.exe in toolchains that ship separate binaries per architecture. Choose OK . TODO — verify Compiler options page Confirm the exact fields on the Compiler options page — whether Tiko asks for the compiler executable or the installation folder, and what additional paths it accepts — and replace the placeholder screenshot with a real capture. Options ▸ Compiler Screenshot of the compiler configuration page Placeholder. Replace with a capture of the Compiler page showing every field. 32-bit and 64-bit FreeBASIC toolchains commonly provide separate 32-bit and 64-bit compilers. Which one you point Tiko at determines the architecture of the programs you build. If your project links against a DLL of a particular architecture, the two must match — a 64-bit program cannot load a 32-bit DLL. Checking that it works Create a new file and type a one-line program: Print \"ok\" : Sleep Save it. Press F5 . The Output panel should show the compiler being invoked, and your program should run. Warning If the build fails immediately with a message about the compiler not being found, the path is wrong — the error is about Tiko's configuration, not your code. See Troubleshooting . Compiler switches Switches reach the compiler from three places, which combine: The active build configuration — the reusable set, such as debug or release. The project's own compiler options — switches specific to this project. Anything Tiko must add itself, such as the output file name. Tip Compile › Command Line… shows the exact command line Tiko will use. When a build does something you did not expect, look there first — it usually settles the question immediately. Related topics Building and running Build configurations Project options"},{"u":"building.html","t":"Building and running","s":"Building Programs","k":"build compile execute run rebuild all quick run run executable compile module command line f5 autosave","b":"The build commands and what each is for, saving before a build, and what happens while a build runs. Tiko's build commands all live on the Compile menu and all report into the Output panel's Compiler tab. The commands Command Shortcut What it does Use when Build and Execute F5 Compiles the project, then runs the result if the compile succeeded. Almost always. This is the main command. Compile Ctrl + F5 Compiles without running. You want to check that it builds, without launching anything. Rebuild All Ctrl + Alt + F5 Rebuilds everything from scratch, ignoring anything already built. After changing compiler switches, or when a build behaves inconsistently. Compile Module Ctrl + F7 Compiles just the current module. Checking one file compiles, without building the whole project. Quick Run Ctrl + Shift + F5 Compiles and runs the current file alone, ignoring the project. Testing a scratch file or a small experiment. Run Executable Shift + F5 Runs the last successful build without recompiling. Running again after a build you already did. Command Line… — Shows the compiler command line Tiko will use. Diagnosing why a build behaves unexpectedly. Tip Quick Run is worth remembering. It builds and runs the current file on its own with no project set-up, which makes Tiko a perfectly good scratchpad for trying out a language feature. Saving before a build The Compile autosave option — on by default — saves modified documents automatically before each build. It prevents the classic confusion of building the previous version of a file and wondering why your change had no effect. What happens during a build Modified files are saved, if Compile autosave is on. Tiko assembles the command line from the build configuration, the project options and the file set. The Output panel switches to the Compiler tab and shows the command being run. Compiler output is captured line by line as it arrives. The result is reported. Errors and warnings become clickable rows. If the build succeeded and the command runs the program, it launches. Running your program Your program runs as a separate process. Console programs get their own console window; GUI programs open their own window. Command-line arguments come from the project options — see Project options . Note Because the program is a separate process, closing Tiko does not close it, and a crash in your program cannot take the editor down"},{"u":"build-configurations.html","t":"Build configurations","s":"Building Programs","k":"build configuration debug release compiler switches f7 default configuration shortcut rebuild","b":"Named sets of compiler switches: creating them, giving them shortcuts, switching between them, and why a rebuild follows a switch. A build configuration is a named set of compiler switches you can switch between — most commonly a Debug configuration carrying debug information and a Release one carrying optimisation. Open the manager with File › Settings › Build Configurations… or F7 . What is in a configuration Part Purpose Description The name shown in the menus and the status bar. Compiler switches The switches this configuration passes to the compiler, chosen from a checklist rather than typed. Keyboard shortcut An optional accelerator that activates this configuration. Default marker Marks the configuration used when a project does not specify one. Using the dialog The dialog lists your configurations on the left and their settings on the right. Command What it does Add Creates a new configuration. Delete Removes the selected one. Move up / Move down Reorders the list — the order they appear in menus. The settings side has two pages: General for the description, shortcut and behaviour, and Compiler switches for the switch checklist. Switches that are mutually exclusive are grouped, so choosing one clears the others in that group. Build Configurations Screenshot of the Build Configurations dialog Placeholder. Replace with a capture showing the configuration list, the General page and the switch checklist. Shortcuts and clashes A configuration can have its own keyboard shortcut so you can switch without opening any menu. If a shortcut cannot work — because the key name is not recognised, or because an editor command or user tool already claims it — Tiko simply leaves that configuration unassigned rather than giving you a shortcut that silently never fires. Note Where two things want the same keystroke, the first one to claim it keeps it. Assign a different key to the loser. Switching configuration Click the build configuration field in the status bar. Use the configuration's own keyboard shortcut, if you gave it one. Choose it from the build configuration menu. Warning After switching configuration, use Rebuild All ( Ctrl + Alt + F5 ) rather than an incremental build. Object files produced under the old switches are not compatible with the new ones. Debug builds Debugging needs a build that carries debug information. Tiko ships debug configurations set up "},{"u":"compiler-errors.html","t":"Compiler errors and warnings","s":"Building Programs","k":"compiler errors warnings build failed output panel navigate error click error line number link","b":"Reading build output, jumping to the offending line, the difference between errors and warnings, and the failures you will meet most often. When a build fails, the compiler's messages appear in the Output panel's Compiler tab. Tiko parses them so each becomes a link into your code. Navigating to an error Click any error row to open that file and put the caret on the line the compiler named. Work down the list from the top. Important Fix the first error first, then rebuild. A single early mistake — an unclosed block, a missing include — routinely produces dozens of later errors that vanish the moment the first is fixed. Chasing them individually wastes time. Errors and warnings Kind Meaning Action Error The compiler could not produce output. Must be fixed. Warning The code compiled, but something looks suspicious. Should be read. Warnings often identify real bugs — an uninitialised variable, a suspicious conversion. Tip Build with the compiler's full warning switch turned on in your debug configuration. A warning you never see cannot help you. Reading a message A compiler message normally carries four things: the file it occurred in — not necessarily the one you were editing; the line , and often the column; the severity , error or warning; the text describing the problem. Note The reported line is where the compiler noticed the problem, which is not always where the problem is. An unterminated block is reported at the end of the file, while the actual mistake is wherever the block was opened. Common FreeBASIC build failures Message resembles Usual cause Variable not declared A typo, a missing Dim , or a missing #include for the header that declares it. Expected ‘End If’ / ‘Next’ / ‘End Sub’ A block opened and never closed — look above the reported line. Duplicated definition The same name declared twice, or a header included twice without #include once . File not found An #include path that is wrong, or an include directory missing from the compiler switches. Undefined reference (at link time) A procedure declared but never implemented, or a library not linked. Related topics Output panel Building and running Troubleshooting Debugging — for problems that only appear at run time."},{"u":"debugging.html","t":"Debugging","s":"Debugging","k":"debug debugger start debugging continue break stop step into step over step out run to cursor f6 f10 f11 panes globals locals call stack watch data tip","b":"Running your program under Tiko's debugger: starting and stopping, stepping, the four debugger panes, and inspecting values. Tiko has a real source-level debugger. It runs your program under its own control, so you can stop it at a chosen line, step through it a statement at a time, and look at the value of any variable while it is stopped. The debugger reads the debug information the FreeBASIC compiler emits directly, so the program you debug is the same binary you built — not a differently-generated one. Before you start Select a build configuration that produces debug information — a Debug configuration. Build the project ( Ctrl + F5 ) so the executable is current. Set at least one breakpoint, otherwise the program simply runs to completion. Starting and stopping Command Shortcut What it does Start Debugging F6 Builds if needed, launches your program under the debugger and runs it until it hits a breakpoint. Continue Debugging F6 The same command once a session is running: resumes from where it stopped. Break Ctrl + F6 Interrupts a running program at the next line of your source. Stop Debugging Shift + F6 Ends the session and terminates the program. Note Break stops at the next line of your code, so a program sitting inside a long Sleep or blocked in a library call will not stop until it returns to your source. Stop Debugging works at any time. Stepping Command Shortcut What it does Step F11 Executes the current line. If it calls one of your procedures, stops on that procedure's first line. Step Over F10 Executes the current line, running any call it makes to completion without stopping inside. Step Out Shift + F11 Runs until the current procedure returns, then stops at the caller. Run to Cursor Ctrl + F10 Resumes and stops when execution reaches the line the caret is on — a breakpoint you do not have to set and clear. Tip Step Over ( F10 ) is the one to reach for by default. Use Step ( F11 ) only when you actually want to go inside the call — otherwise you spend your time in code you were not investigating. The line about to execute is marked in the editor, and the marker follows you as you step. The debugger window While debugging, four panes show you the state of your program: Globals gConfig CONFIG_TYPE gAppName \"tiko\" ▸ gBuffer(0..999) [1000 elements] Locals i 12 total 438.75 name \"widget\" ▸ rc RECT Call stack ComputeTotal main.bas:142 ProcessRow mai"},{"u":"breakpoints.html","t":"Breakpoints","s":"Debugging","k":"breakpoint toggle breakpoint f9 delete all breakpoints margin click executable line run to cursor","b":"Setting, clearing and placing breakpoints, why they sometimes move to the next line, and how they survive edits and rebuilds. A breakpoint tells the debugger to stop your program when it reaches a particular line. Everything else in debugging follows from being stopped somewhere useful. Setting a breakpoint Method How Keyboard Put the caret on the line and press F9 . Menu Debug › Toggle Breakpoint Mouse Click the left margin beside the line — if Click toggles breakpoint is enabled in the options. A marker appears in the margin. The same command removes it again. Debug › Delete All Breakpoints clears every breakpoint in the project. Breakpoints on lines that generate no code Important Not every line becomes machine code. Comments, blank lines and bare declarations produce nothing to stop at. Rather than accepting a breakpoint that would silently never fire, Tiko moves it forward to the next line that does generate code. If a breakpoint appears to jump one or two lines when you set it, that is what happened — and it is doing you a favour. Breakpoints and rebuilding Breakpoints are stored by file and line, so they survive editing and rebuilding. Inserting lines above a breakpoint moves the marker with the code, because the marker lives in the editor's line markers rather than at a fixed line number. Note Breakpoints set before a session starts are armed when the program launches, so you can prepare the breakpoints you want and then press F6 once. Run to Cursor Ctrl + F10 resumes and stops when execution reaches the caret's line. It is the right tool when you want to stop somewhere just once — no breakpoint to set, and none to remember to remove afterwards. Choosing where to break Just before the suspect code , not on it — so you can inspect the inputs before they are used. Inside the branch you doubt . If a breakpoint in a branch never fires, you have learned that the branch is not taken, which is often the bug. At the top of a procedure when you want to know whether it is called at all, and with what. Tip Stopping inside a loop that runs thousands of times gets tedious quickly. Break at the line after the loop and inspect the result, or put the breakpoint inside an If that only matches the case you care about. Related topics Debugging Watches and the call stack Display and view options — the margin settings."},{"u":"watches.html","t":"Watches and the call stack","s":"Debugging","k":"watch watches expression call stack frame locals globals changed values array structure expand dynamic array scope","b":"Tracking expressions across steps, reading changed-value highlighting, and using the call stack to see how execution arrived where it is. While your program is stopped, the debugger panes let you look at anything in scope. This page covers the two panes that need most explanation: the watch list and the call stack. Watches A watch is an expression the debugger evaluates and displays every time the program stops. Use one for a value you want to follow across many steps, rather than hunting for it in the Locals pane each time. Click the <click to add a watch> row in the Watch pane. Type the expression — a variable name, a structure member, an array element. Press Enter . Action How Edit a watch Click it, or press F2 on it, and type. Delete a watch Click the × in the delete column at the end of the row. Expand a structure or array Click its twisty. Note A watch that is not in scope at the current line cannot be evaluated, and the pane says so rather than showing a misleading value. Step into a procedure where it is in scope and it starts reporting again. Tip Watch an expression, not just a variable — rows(i).total tells you far more inside a loop than i and rows separately. Changed values Values that changed since the program last stopped are drawn in a distinct colour. When you are stepping through a loop this turns a wall of numbers into a short list of things that actually moved. The highlight is cleared when the program resumes, so each stop shows what changed since the previous one. The call stack The call stack shows how execution reached the current line: the procedure you are in, the one that called it, and so on out to your program's entry point. Click a frame to look at that point in the chain. The editor jumps to the call site and the Locals pane switches to that frame's variables — which is how you answer \"what were the arguments when this was called?\" Important Clicking a frame changes only what you are looking at . It does not unwind the stack or change where execution will resume — that is still the innermost frame. Reading a stack after a crash When a program fails inside a library routine, the top frames are often code you did not write. Read down the stack to the first frame in your own source: that is the call that passed in whatever caused the failure, and it is where to look first. Arrays and structures Structures expand to their members, a"},{"u":"themes.html","t":"Themes","s":"Customization","k":"theme themes dark light colour color scheme default_dark studio slate sepia contrast arctic midnight neon roles keys accent","b":"Choosing among the fourteen bundled colour themes, and how Tiko's two-layer role and key model makes themes short and consistent. A theme sets every colour in Tiko — the editor's syntax colours, and the panels, menus, dialogs, tabs and status bar as well. Changing theme changes the whole application, not just the code area. Choosing a theme Open File › Settings › Themes… , or press Ctrl + Shift + T . Pick a theme from the list. Choose OK . The interface redraws immediately. The bundled themes Tiko ships fourteen themes, in matched light and dark families: Theme Character default_dark / default_light Tiko's own palette. The starting point. studio_dark / studio_light Familiar to anyone coming from a mainstream code editor. slate_dark / slate_light Cool blue-grey, moderate contrast. sepia_dark / sepia_light Warm, low-blue tones — easy on the eyes over long sessions. contrast_dark / contrast_light High contrast, for difficult lighting or reduced vision. arctic A cool, muted light palette. retro_warm Warm earthy colours. midnight A deep, saturated dark theme. neon High-saturation accents on a dark background. Note Themes are files: each is a .theme file in settings\\themes . Adding a theme is copying a file into that folder; sharing one is sending that file to someone. How a theme is built Tiko's themes work in two layers, which is what keeps them short and internally consistent. Layer What it is Roles About twenty semantic slots — background, accent, accent foreground, and the syntax slots such as keyword, string, comment and number. A role says what a colour means . Keys Individual colour settings for specific elements — editor.divider , panel.text.backcolor and so on. A key that is not given a value falls back to its role. Because of that fallback, a theme that defines only the roles is complete and valid. Several of the bundled themes are barely thirty lines long for exactly this reason. You override individual keys only where you want to depart from what the role implies. Important Two roles are deliberately separate and easy to confuse. Accent is a fill colour — the background of a selected tab or a highlighted row. Accent foreground is ink — text drawn on top of that fill. Using one where the other belongs produces text that is invisible in one theme and fine in another. Making a theme your own Use the theme editor to adjust colours — see The theme editor ."},{"u":"theme-editor.html","t":"The theme editor","s":"Customization","k":"theme editor colour picker color picker roles palette keys rgb alpha web system custom swatch save theme","b":"Editing colours: the palette of semantic roles, the per-element key pages, and the colour picker. The theme editor lets you change any colour in Tiko and see the result immediately. Open it with File › Settings › Themes… or Ctrl + Shift + T . The palette page The first page shows the theme's roles — the semantic colours everything else inherits from. Changing a role changes every element that has not explicitly overridden it, which is the efficient way to retune a theme: adjust a dozen roles rather than a hundred keys. A role the current theme has not chosen for itself is marked as using its built-in default, so you can see at a glance how much of the theme is genuinely authored. The key pages The remaining pages group individual colour keys by the part of the interface they affect — the editor, the panels, the tabs, the output window, tooltips, dialogs. Every row shows, per colour channel, which role it currently inherits from, so you always know what a value will fall back to if you clear it. Theme editor Screenshot of the theme editor Placeholder. Replace with a capture showing the key list, the colour picker and the R/G/B entry rows. Choosing a colour Clicking a colour button opens the colour picker, which has three tabs: Tab Offers Web A matrix of tints and shades, swept across hue and lightness. System The named colours Windows itself defines. Custom A scrolling list of named colours. Below the matrix, the picker shows the initial and current colours side by side and offers R, G, B and alpha entry fields for exact values. Double-clicking a swatch chooses it and closes the picker in one action. Note The picker is modal: the colour is applied when you choose OK , and cancelling leaves the previous colour untouched. Saving a theme Save your changes to a new .theme file rather than overwriting a shipped one, so that an update cannot replace your work. TODO — verify theme save commands Confirm the exact save, save-as, rename and delete commands in the Themes dialog of the shipping build, and document them step by step. Practical advice Change roles first. Most of what looks wrong about a theme is a role, not a key. Check both text and background. A colour that reads well on the editor background may be illegible on a panel. Look at the hover and selection states. These are two backgrounds compared with each other rather than ink against a background, and th"},{"u":"editor-appearance.html","t":"Fonts and editor appearance","s":"Customization","k":"font editor font consolas font size character set charset line spacing extra space monospace zoom dpi compact menus","b":"Choosing the editor font, size, character set and line spacing, and how zoom differs from the configured size. Font settings live in File › Settings › Options… . They apply to the editing surface; the rest of the interface uses the system font. Editor font Font name The typeface used in the editor. The default is Consolas . Choose from the scrolling list, which previews each font in its own face. Font size In points. The default is 11. Character set The character set the font is requested with. Leave this at its default unless you are working with a script that needs a specific one. Extra line spacing Additional space between lines, in pixels. A small amount — the default is 2 — makes dense code noticeably easier to read. Important Use a monospaced font. Code alignment, indent guides, the right-edge marker and column selection all assume every character is the same width. Consolas, Cascadia Mono, DejaVu Sans Mono and Courier New all qualify. Tip If your font has a version with programming ligatures, it can make operators easier to read — but check that it does not disturb column alignment before adopting it for real work. Zoom versus font size Ctrl + + and Ctrl + - zoom the display temporarily; Ctrl + 0 resets it. The configured font size is unchanged, which is why zoom does not persist between sessions and the font setting does. Interface density Compact menus draws menu rows at a tighter height. Panel and Output panel sizes are set by dragging their splitters, and are remembered. The Explorer can sit on either side of the window. High-DPI displays Tiko scales its interface to the display's DPI setting, so it stays legible on high-resolution screens. Colours and fonts behave the same at any scale. TODO — confirm per-monitor DPI behaviour Document any per-monitor DPI behaviour worth knowing about — for example what happens when the window is dragged between monitors with different scaling factors. Related topics Themes Display and view options Configuration reference"},{"u":"keyboard-customization.html","t":"Customizing keyboard shortcuts","s":"Customization","k":"keyboard shortcuts customize rebind remap assign key conflict disabled default keybindings.ini reset accelerator","b":"Rebinding any command, resolving conflicts, disabling a default, and where your bindings are stored. Every command in Tiko can be rebound. Open the Keyboard Shortcuts dialog with File › Settings › Keyboard Shortcuts… or Ctrl + K . The dialog The dialog lists every command with four columns — the category, the command, its current shortcut and its description — above a filter box that searches all four. Type part of a command name or a key name to narrow the list. Button What it does Modify Opens the assignment dialog for the selected command. Reset Restores the selected command to its default shortcut. OK Saves every change and rebuilds the accelerator table. Cancel Discards every change. Nothing you did in the dialog takes effect. Note The dialog works on a copy of your bindings, so Cancel really cancels — you can experiment freely and back out. Assigning a shortcut Select the command and choose Modify . Either press the keystroke you want in the capture field, or build it by hand: toggle the Ctrl, Alt and Shift switches and pick the key from the list. If the keystroke is already taken, the dialog says so and refuses it rather than silently stealing it from the other command. Choose OK in the assignment dialog, then OK in the main dialog to commit. Assign shortcut Screenshot of the shortcut assignment dialog Placeholder. Replace with a capture showing the capture field, the modifier switches and the key list. Conflicts A keystroke can only do one thing. Tiko checks for conflicts across editor commands, user tools and build configuration shortcuts, and marks any conflict it finds in the list. Where a conflict is inherited from an older settings file, the first command to claim the keystroke keeps it and the others show as unassigned. Reassign the loser to something free. Disabling a default A default shortcut can be suppressed without being replaced — useful when a keystroke conflicts with something outside Tiko, such as a global hotkey from another application. A suppressed default shows in the list marked as disabled. Where bindings are stored Your changes go into settings\\keybindings.ini . Only your overrides are stored — the defaults live in the program, so the file stays small and readable: ini settings\\keybindings.ini Copy IDM_FILESAVE:Ctrl+S IDM_DUPLICATELINE:Ctrl+Shift+D IDM_BOOKMARKTOGGLE(DISABLED): Each line names a command, then the keystroke you "},{"u":"user-tools.html","t":"User tools","s":"Customization","k":"user tools external tools run program parameters substitution working folder shortcut menu","b":"Defining external programs you can launch from the menu or a shortcut, with parameters substituted from the current file or project. A user tool is an external program you can run from inside Tiko — a formatter, a version-control command, a packaging script, a documentation generator. Tools appear on File › User Tools and can have their own keyboard shortcuts. Defining a tool Open File › User Tools… . Choose Add . Fill in the description, the program to run, its parameters and its working folder. Optionally assign a keyboard shortcut. Choose OK . Field Purpose Description The text shown on the User Tools menu. Program The executable to run. Parameters The command line passed to it. Substitution codes let you pass the current file, project or selection — see below. Working folder The directory the program starts in. Shortcut An optional accelerator. The Parameters field carries a tooltip listing the substitution codes available — the current file name, its folder, the project file, the line number and so on. TODO — enumerate parameter substitution codes List every parameter substitution code the shipping build supports, with an example of each, and replace this note with that table. User Tools Screenshot of the User Tools dialog Placeholder. Replace with a capture showing the tool list and the detail fields. Ordering and shortcuts Reorder tools with the up and down chevrons — the order in the list is the order on the menu. A tool's shortcut is checked against every editor command and every other tool, and one that would conflict is refused rather than silently overriding. Ideas for tools Run git status or git diff on the current project folder. Open the project folder in Explorer. Run a packaging or deployment batch file. Run an external linter or static analyser over the current file. Open the current file in another editor for a specialised task. Related topics Customizing keyboard shortcuts User Tools dialog reference"},{"u":"localization.html","t":"Localization","s":"Customization","k":"localization language translation lang file english fallback interface language phrase editor translate","b":"Choosing the interface language, how language files and the English fallback work, and editing or creating a translation. Tiko's interface text is loaded from a language file, so it can be displayed in another language — or reworded to your taste. Choosing a language Open File › Settings › Options… . Select the Localization page. Select a language from the list and choose Select . The marked language is the active one. Choose OK . Note A language change takes effect the next time Tiko starts, since the interface is built from the language file as the program loads. How language files work Each language is a .lang file in settings\\languages . Every piece of interface text has a numeric identifier, and a language file maps identifiers to text. Important english.lang is the canonical file and the fallback for every other language. If a translation leaves an entry blank, the English text is used for that item — so a partial translation produces a mixed interface rather than blank menus. Editing a translation The Localization page has two modes. Normally it lists the available languages with commands beside them; choosing Edit replaces that list with the phrase editor for the language you picked, giving the phrase list the whole page. Command What it does Select Makes that language the active interface language. New Creates a new, empty language file. Edit Opens that language's phrases for editing. Delete Removes a language file. Back Returns from the phrase editor to the language list. In the phrase editor, each row shows the phrase identifier, the English text and your translation. Edit the translation column in place: double-click it, or press Enter or F2 on the row. Important Selecting a language and editing one are different actions. Opening French to correct a typo does not switch Tiko into French — only Select does that. Warning Saving a language file from the editor rewrites the whole file. Comments and translator attribution at the top of a hand-written .lang file are not preserved. Keep a copy of any file you care about before editing it here. Creating a new translation Choose New and give the language a name and a file name. Choose Edit and work through the phrase list, filling in translations. Leave anything you have not translated blank — the English text is used for it. Choose Select to make it active, then restart Tiko. Related topics Settings and "},{"u":"settings-files.html","t":"Settings and configuration files","s":"Customization","k":"settings.ini configuration files keybindings.ini portable backup export import reset defaults registry program files multiple installations","b":"Where Tiko stores everything it remembers, how to edit those files, back them up, move them between machines and reset them. Everything Tiko remembers is a plain file next to tiko.exe . There is nothing in the registry and nothing in your user profile, which is what makes Tiko portable. The files File Holds settings\\settings.ini Every editor setting: fonts, colours, editing behaviour, window geometry, panel layout, the compiler path and the recent-file lists. settings\\keybindings.ini Your keyboard shortcut overrides. Only overrides — defaults live in the program. settings\\default.tiko The untitled workspace, so loose files are restored on start-up. settings\\themes\\*.theme Colour themes. settings\\languages\\*.lang Interface translations. settings\\keywords\\*.txt Syntax highlighting keyword lists, which also supply autocomplete entries and canonical keyword spellings. The settings file settings.ini is a conventional INI file, grouped into sections. It is written when you choose OK in a settings dialog and when Tiko exits. ini settings\\settings.ini (extract) Copy [Editor] SyntaxHighlighting=-1 AutoComplete=-1 TabSize=4 TabIndentSpaces=-1 EditorFontname=Consolas EditorFontsize=11 Theme=default_dark.theme LocalizationFile=english.lang [Startup] StartupMaximized=-1 ShowPanel=1 ShowPanelWidth=236 ShowOutputPanel=0 Important Boolean settings use the BASIC convention: -1 is true and 0 is false . A setting written as 1 is not what a true value looks like here. Every key is documented in the configuration reference . Editing settings by hand Warning Close Tiko before editing settings.ini . The editor writes the whole file when it exits, so changes made while it is running are overwritten. Most settings are better changed through the options dialog, which validates what you enter. Editing the file directly is useful for bulk changes and for values you want to copy between installations. Backing up, and moving to another machine Copy the settings folder. That is the entire backup — settings, shortcuts, themes, languages and keyword lists. To transfer Copy Everything The whole settings folder. Just your shortcuts settings\\keybindings.ini Just a theme The .theme file from settings\\themes Just your editor preferences settings\\settings.ini — but see the note below. Note settings.ini also holds window positions and the recent-file lists, which are specific to a machine. Copying"},{"u":"keyboard-shortcuts.html","t":"Keyboard shortcuts","s":"Reference","k":"keyboard shortcuts accelerators hotkeys keys reference print cheat sheet default bindings","b":"Every default keyboard shortcut in Tiko, grouped by category and ready to print. Every shortcut below is the factory default. All of them can be changed — see Customizing keyboard shortcuts . A command shown with a dash has no default shortcut, and is a good candidate if you need a free keystroke. Tip This page prints cleanly. Use your browser's Print command for a reference sheet without the navigation. The ten to learn first If you learn nothing else, learn these. They cover the great majority of daily use. Shortcut Command Why Ctrl + S Save Constantly. Ctrl + F Find The most-used navigation tool in any editor. F3 Find Next Repeats the search without the Find bar open. F12 Goto Definition Jump to where anything is defined. Alt + Left Go Back Return from that jump. Ctrl + P Search Symbol Jump anywhere in the project by name. F5 Build And Execute The whole build cycle in one key. F9 Toggle Breakpoint The start of every debugging session. F6 Start Debugging Run under the debugger. Ctrl + B Toggle side panel Give the editor the whole window. File commands Creating, opening, saving and closing documents, plus the settings dialogs. Shortcut Command Description Ctrl + N New Create a new, empty document. Ctrl + O Open… Open one or more existing files. — Open File As Template… Open a file's contents as a new untitled document, leaving the original untouched. Ctrl + W Close Close the active document. It stays in the project. Ctrl + Shift + W Close All Close every open document. Ctrl + S Save Save the active document. Prompts for a name if it has never been saved. — Save As… Save under a new name and continue editing the new file. Ctrl + Shift + S Save All Save every modified document. — Rename… Rename the file on disk and update the project. — Duplicate Create a copy of the file and add it to the project. Ctrl + , Options… Open the main options dialog. Ctrl + Shift + T Themes… Choose and edit colour themes. Ctrl + K Keyboard Shortcuts… Remap any command. F7 Build Configurations… Manage named sets of compiler switches. — User Tools… Define external programs to run from the menu. Alt + F4 Exit Close Tiko, prompting to save modified documents. Edit commands Undo and redo, the clipboard, line operations, commenting, case conversion and the code formatter. Shortcut Command Description Ctrl + Z Undo Undo the last change. Ctrl + Shift + Z Redo Reapply an undone change. Ctr"},{"u":"menu-reference.html","t":"Menu reference","s":"Reference","k":"menu reference file edit search view project compile debug help commands submenu open recent settings user tools format recent projects","b":"Every menu and every command it holds, with shortcuts, descriptions and the submenus that rebuild themselves. Every command on every menu, with its keyboard shortcut and a description. Commands whose names end in an ellipsis open a dialog. File menu Document lifecycle — creating, opening, saving and closing — plus the settings dialogs, user tools and Exit. Command Shortcut Description New Ctrl + N Create a new, empty document. Open… Ctrl + O Open one or more existing files. Open File As Template… — Open a file's contents as a new untitled document, leaving the original untouched. Close Ctrl + W Close the active document. It stays in the project. Close All Ctrl + Shift + W Close every open document. Save Ctrl + S Save the active document. Prompts for a name if it has never been saved. Save As… — Save under a new name and continue editing the new file. Save All Ctrl + Shift + S Save every modified document. Rename… — Rename the file on disk and update the project. Duplicate — Create a copy of the file and add it to the project. Options… Ctrl + , Open the main options dialog. Themes… Ctrl + Shift + T Choose and edit colour themes. Keyboard Shortcuts… Ctrl + K Remap any command. Build Configurations… F7 Manage named sets of compiler switches. User Tools… — Define external programs to run from the menu. Exit Alt + F4 Close Tiko, prompting to save modified documents. Edit menu Undo and redo, the clipboard, line operations, commenting, selection and the Format submenu. Command Shortcut Description Undo Ctrl + Z Undo the last change. Redo Ctrl + Shift + Z Reapply an undone change. Cut Ctrl + X Cut the selection, or the whole line if nothing is selected. Copy Ctrl + C Copy the selection, or the whole line if nothing is selected. Paste Ctrl + V Paste the clipboard as plain text. Delete Line Ctrl + Y Delete the current line. Duplicate Line Ctrl + D Insert a copy of the current line below it. Move Line Up Alt + Up Swap the current line or selection with the line above. Move Line Down Alt + Down Swap the current line or selection with the line below. Comment Block Ctrl + / Comment out the selected lines. UnComment Block Ctrl + Shift + / Remove comment marks from the selected lines. Select Line Ctrl + L Select the whole current line. Select All Ctrl + A Select the entire document. Indent Block Tab Indent every line of a multi-line selection. UnIndent Block Shift + Tab Rem"},{"u":"command-reference.html","t":"Command reference","s":"Reference","k":"command reference command id idm identifier keybindings.ini menu location shortcut disabled","b":"Every editor command with its internal identifier, default shortcut and menu location — and how to use identifiers in the keybindings file. Every command in Tiko, with its internal identifier, its default shortcut and where it lives. The identifier is the name used in settings\\keybindings.ini — useful when editing that file by hand or comparing configurations. Note Commands are listed by category, matching the grouping in the Keyboard Shortcuts dialog. File Command Command ID Shortcut Menu location New IDM_FILENEW Ctrl + N File › New Open… IDM_FILEOPEN Ctrl + O File › Open… Open File As Template… IDM_FILEOPENASTEMPLATE — File › Open File As Template… Close IDM_FILECLOSE Ctrl + W File › Close Close All IDM_FILECLOSEALL Ctrl + Shift + W File › Close All Save IDM_FILESAVE Ctrl + S File › Save Save As… IDM_FILESAVEAS — File › Save As… Save All IDM_FILESAVEALL Ctrl + Shift + S File › Save All Rename… IDM_FILERENAME — File › Rename… Duplicate IDM_FILEDUPLICATE — File › Duplicate Options… IDM_OPTIONSDIALOG Ctrl + , File › Settings › Options… Themes… IDM_THEMES Ctrl + Shift + T File › Settings › Themes… Keyboard Shortcuts… IDM_KEYBOARDSHORTCUTS Ctrl + K File › Settings › Keyboard Shortcuts… Build Configurations… IDM_BUILDCONFIG F7 File › Settings › Build Configurations… User Tools… IDM_USERTOOLSDIALOG — File › User Tools… Exit IDM_EXIT Alt + F4 File › Exit Edit Command Command ID Shortcut Menu location Undo IDM_UNDO Ctrl + Z Edit › Undo Redo IDM_REDO Ctrl + Shift + Z Edit › Redo Cut IDM_CUT Ctrl + X Edit › Cut Copy IDM_COPY Ctrl + C Edit › Copy Paste IDM_PASTE Ctrl + V Edit › Paste Delete Line IDM_DELETELINE Ctrl + Y Edit › Delete Line Duplicate Line IDM_DUPLICATELINE Ctrl + D Edit › Duplicate Line Move Line Up IDM_MOVELINEUP Alt + Up Edit › Move Line Up Move Line Down IDM_MOVELINEDOWN Alt + Down Edit › Move Line Down Comment Block IDM_COMMENTBLOCK Ctrl + / Edit › Comment Block UnComment Block IDM_UNCOMMENTBLOCK Ctrl + Shift + / Edit › UnComment Block Select Line IDM_SELECTLINE Ctrl + L Edit › Select Line Select All IDM_SELECTALL Ctrl + A Edit › Select All Indent Block IDM_INDENTBLOCK Tab No menu item UnIndent Block IDM_UNINDENTBLOCK Shift + Tab No menu item Insert File IDM_INSERTFILE Ctrl + I No menu item Uppercase IDM_TOUPPERCASE Ctrl + Alt + U No menu item Lowercase IDM_TOLOWERCASE Ctrl + Alt + L No menu item Mixed case IDM_TOMIXEDCASE Ctrl + Alt + X No menu item"},{"u":"configuration-reference.html","t":"Configuration reference","s":"Reference","k":"configuration reference settings.ini options keys defaults values editor startup formatter fmt boolean -1 0","b":"Every setting in settings.ini: what it does, its default, the values it accepts and the settings it relates to. Every setting Tiko stores in settings\\settings.ini . Most are set through File › Settings › Options… ; they are documented here so you can read the file, compare two installations, or edit it in bulk. Important Booleans follow the BASIC convention: -1 means true and 0 means false . Warning Close Tiko before editing the file by hand. It rewrites the whole file on exit, which discards anything you changed while it was running. [Editor] — general behaviour AskExit Prompt for confirmation when closing Tiko. Default 0 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) AutoSaveFiles Save modified documents automatically at an interval. Default 0 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) Related AutoSaveInterval AutoSaveInterval Minutes between automatic saves. Default 10 Values A whole number of minutes Related AutoSaveFiles RestoreSession Reopen the workspace that was active when Tiko last closed. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) Related LastActiveProjectFile LastActiveProjectFile The project reopened at start-up. Maintained by Tiko; you do not normally edit it. Default (empty) Values A path to a .tiko file MultipleInstances Allow more than one copy of Tiko to run at once. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) CompactMenus Draw menu rows at a tighter height. Default 0 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) CheckUpdates Check whether a newer version is available. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) DetectExternalFileChanges Notice when an open file is changed by another program and offer to reload it. Default 0 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) LocalizationFile The interface language file to load. Default english.lang Values A file name in settings\\languages Related Localization Theme The colour theme to load. Default default_dark.theme Values A file name in settings\\themes Related Themes ExplorerPositionRight Dock the side panel on the right of the window. Default 0 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) [Editor] — code intelligence SyntaxHighlighting Colour code according to its syntax. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) Related Syntax highlighting Codetips Show parameter hints when you open a call's parenthesis. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (false) Related Autocomplete and code tips AutoComplete Offer a completion list as you type identifiers. Default -1 Values -1 (true) or 0 (fa"},{"u":"dialog-reference.html","t":"Dialog reference","s":"Reference","k":"dialog reference options themes build configurations keyboard shortcuts user tools format options project options find replace find in project about controls","b":"Every dialog box in Tiko, control by control, with links to the topic that explains each in context. Every dialog in Tiko and what its controls do. Dialogs edit a working copy of your settings, so Cancel always discards every change made since the dialog opened. Options Opened with File › Settings › Options… or Ctrl + , . Eight pages, listed down the left. Page Covers General Start-up behaviour, autosave, update checking, multiple instances and menu density. Code Editor Line numbers, margins, current-line highlighting, indent guides, the right-edge marker, tabs and indentation. Advanced Code Editor Code tips, autocomplete, character auto-completion, auto-indentation and its For/Next option, brace and occurrence highlighting. Compiler Where your FreeBASIC compiler is installed. Localization The interface language, and the phrase editor. Colors Editor and interface colours. Keywords The syntax highlighting keyword lists. Font The editor font name, size, character set and extra line spacing. OK applies and saves; Cancel discards everything. TODO — enumerate Options pages and controls Confirm the exact page names and their order in the shipping build's options dialog, and document each page control by control. Themes Opened with File › Settings › Themes… or Ctrl + Shift + T . Control Purpose Theme list The themes available in settings\\themes . Palette page The theme's semantic roles. Changing one changes everything that inherits from it. Key pages Individual colour keys, grouped by the part of the interface they affect, each showing the role it falls back to. Colour button Opens the colour picker for that key or role. R / G / B / A fields Enter an exact colour value. See The theme editor . Build Configurations Opened with File › Settings › Build Configurations… or F7 . Control Purpose Configuration list Your configurations, with a marker on the default. Add / Delete Create or remove a configuration. Move up / Move down Reorder the list, which is the order shown in menus. General page Description, keyboard shortcut and behaviour options. Compiler switches page A checklist of compiler switches. Mutually exclusive switches are grouped. Shortcut An optional accelerator. One that cannot work is left unassigned rather than silently failing. See Build configurations . Keyboard Shortcuts Opened with File › Settings › Keyboard Shortcuts… or Ctrl + K . Control Purpose Fil"},{"u":"tutorial.html","t":"Tutorial","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lessons course learn beginner training walkthrough","b":"A ten-lesson course that takes you from installing Tiko to using its advanced editing features, with exercises throughout. This tutorial teaches Tiko from the beginning. Each lesson builds on the last, takes 10–20 minutes, and ends with exercises that make the material stick. Work through it in order the first time. You will build one small FreeBASIC program across the whole course, so by the end you will have used the editor the way you will use it every day: writing code, searching it, organising it into a project, building it, debugging it and tuning the editor to your taste. Before you start Tiko unpacked into a folder you can write to. A FreeBASIC compiler installed, if you want to do lessons 6 and 7. Lessons 1–5 and 8–10 need no compiler at all. About two hours in total, or one lesson at a time. Note In a hurry? The Quick Start covers the same ground in 25 minutes without the exercises. The lessons 1 — Opening Tiko Install, launch, and learn the parts of the window. 10 minutes. 2 — Your first file Create, type, save and reopen a file. 10 minutes. 3 — Editing text Selections, clipboard, line operations and column mode. 20 minutes. 4 — Searching Find, Replace, Find in Project and regular expressions. 15 minutes. 5 — Projects Turn loose files into a named project. 15 minutes. 6 — Building a program Configure the compiler, build, run and fix errors. 20 minutes. 7 — Debugging Breakpoints, stepping, watches and the call stack. 20 minutes. 8 — Customization Themes, fonts and keyboard shortcuts. 15 minutes. 9 — Productivity shortcuts Navigation, autocomplete and the formatter. 15 minutes. 10 — Advanced editing Multiple selections, folding, split views and user tools. 20 minutes. Conventions used Style Means Ctrl + S Press these keys together. File › Save A menu path: open File, then choose Save. Print \"hi\" Text to type, or code to read. OK A button or control to click."},{"u":"lesson-01.html","t":"Lesson 1 — Opening Tiko","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 1 install launch window regions panels","b":"Install and launch Tiko, and learn the parts of its window. Goal: Install and launch Tiko, and learn the parts of its window. Time: 10 minutes Before you start: Nothing — this is the starting point. What you will learn Where to unpack Tiko, and why the location matters. The seven regions of the main window. How to show, hide and resize the panels. How to switch between the three side panel views. Launch Tiko Unpack the distribution into a folder you can write to — C:\\tiko , for example. Not C:\\Program Files : Tiko keeps its settings beside the executable and that folder is protected. Double-click tiko.exe . The main window opens on an empty untitled workspace. Note There is no installer and nothing is written to the registry. Removing Tiko later is deleting the folder. Find the seven regions Locate each of these before going on. You will use all of them. Region Where Try it Menu bar Across the top. Open the File menu, then press Esc . Panel icon strip The narrow column of buttons at the side. Hover a button to see its tooltip. Side panel Beside the icon strip. Press Ctrl + B twice to hide and show it. Document tabs Above the editor. Empty until you open a file. Editor The centre. Click in it and type something. Output panel Below the editor. Press Ctrl + F9 twice. Status bar Along the bottom. Watch the line and column change as you move the caret. Switch the side panel views The side panel shows one of three views. Try each: Ctrl + F4 — the Explorer, showing your workspace files. F4 — the function list for the current file. Shift + F4 — your bookmarks. They are all empty for now. They fill up as you work. Resize things Point at the boundary between the side panel and the editor. The cursor becomes a resize arrow. Drag it to make the panel wider, then narrower. Do the same with the boundary above the Output panel. Choose View › Restore Main Window Size to put everything back. Expected result Tiko is running, you can name every region of the window, and you can show, hide and resize the panels. Common mistakes Symptom Cause and fix Tiko starts but has no colours and menus look wrong. You ran a copy of tiko.exe on its own. Run it from the folder you unpacked, where its settings folder is. Settings do not stick between runs. Tiko is installed somewhere it cannot write. Move the folder out of C:\\Program Files . The side panel will not reappear. Press Ctrl + B , o"},{"u":"lesson-02.html","t":"Lesson 2 — Creating your first file","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 2 new file save save as open recent close","b":"Create, type, save and reopen a file, and understand what Save As does. Goal: Create, type, save and reopen a file, and understand what Save As does. Time: 10 minutes Before you start: Lesson 1. What you will learn Creating a document and saving it for the first time. The difference between Save and Save As. Closing a document and reopening it from Open Recent. Why your files come back when you restart Tiko. Create a file Press Ctrl + N . A tab appears, named Untitled . Type the program below. Watch the keywords colour themselves as you type. fb greet.bas Copy ' greet.bas - Lesson 2 Dim As String name Print \"What is your name?\" Input name Print \"Hello, \"; name; \"!\" Sleep Tip Type it rather than pasting it. You will see auto-indentation and syntax highlighting working, which is the point of the exercise. Save it Press Ctrl + S . Because the file has never been saved, the Save As dialog opens. Create a folder for this tutorial — C:\\tiko-tutorial is fine. Name the file greet.bas and save. The tab caption changes from Untitled to greet.bas . Now change something — add a comment at the end — and press Ctrl + S again. It saves straight away with no dialog, because the file now has a name. Save As, and what it really does Choose File › Save As… . Save it as greet2.bas . Look at the tab: it now reads greet2.bas . Important After Save As you are editing the new file. The original is closed and left as it was at its last save. This surprises people who expect Save As to make a backup copy and keep working on the original — it does the opposite. Close and reopen Press Ctrl + W to close the document. Open File › Open Recent . Both files are listed. Choose greet.bas . It opens where you left it. Note Tiko also reopens whatever you had open when you last closed it — even though you have not created a project. Those loose files already are a project, an untitled one. Lesson 5 covers this properly. Expected result Two files in your tutorial folder, one of them open, and both listed under Open Recent. Common mistakes Symptom Cause and fix Pressing Ctrl + S opens a dialog every time. You are pressing Ctrl + Shift + S (Save All), or the document genuinely has not been saved yet. The original file did not change after Save As. That is correct behaviour. Save As switches you to editing the new file. The tab shows a modified marker that will not clear. The file has unsaved change"},{"u":"lesson-03.html","t":"Lesson 3 — Editing text","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 3 editing selection clipboard duplicate move line column mode comment","b":"Select, move, duplicate and comment code efficiently, including column editing. Goal: Select, move, duplicate and comment code efficiently, including column editing. Time: 20 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–2. What you will learn Selecting by word, line and block. Duplicating, deleting and moving lines with no selection. Editing several lines at once with column mode. Commenting and uncommenting blocks. Open greet.bas and replace its contents with this: fb inventory.bas Copy ' inventory.bas - Lesson 3 Dim As String item1, item2, item3 Dim As Integer qty1, qty2, qty3 item1 = \"bolt\" item2 = \"nut\" item3 = \"washer\" qty1 = 100 qty2 = 250 qty3 = 75 Print item1, qty1 Print item2, qty2 Print item3, qty3 Sleep Save it as inventory.bas . Selections Double-click washer . The word is selected. Triple-click the same line. The whole line is selected. Click at the start of the first Print line, then hold Shift and press ↓ twice. Three lines are selected. Press Ctrl + A to select everything, then click once to clear it. Line operations These work with no selection at all, which is what makes them fast. Put the caret anywhere on the qty2 = 250 line. Press Ctrl + D . A copy appears below. Press Ctrl + Y . The duplicate is removed again. Press Alt + ↑ twice. The line moves up past its neighbours. Press Alt + ↓ twice to put it back. Press Ctrl + L to select the whole line, then Ctrl + C to copy it. Tip With nothing selected, Ctrl + X and Ctrl + C act on the whole line. To move a line, press Ctrl + X , click where you want it, and press Ctrl + V . Column editing This is the technique worth practising until it is automatic. Hold Alt and drag straight down the left edge of the three Print lines, without moving right. You now have three carets stacked vertically. Type ' — an apostrophe and a space. All three lines are commented at once. Press Ctrl + Z to undo it. Now hold Alt and drag across the qty in all three qty1/2/3 declarations to select a rectangle. Type amount . All three change together. Press Ctrl + Z to undo. Note A zero-width column selection gives you a caret per line, which inserts. A column with width selects a rectangle, which replaces. Commenting Select the three Print lines. Press Ctrl + / . All three are commented. Press Ctrl + Shift + / . The comments are removed. This is easier than the column trick for commenting, and it knows the language's comment charac"},{"u":"lesson-04.html","t":"Lesson 4 — Searching","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 4 find replace search project regular expression whole word","b":"Find and replace text in a file, search a whole project, and use a simple regular expression. Goal: Find and replace text in a file, search a whole project, and use a simple regular expression. Time: 15 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–3. What you will learn Finding text and repeating a search with F3 . What Whole word and Match case actually change. Replacing one match at a time, and all at once. Searching every file in the project. The idea behind regular expressions. Keep inventory.bas open for this lesson. Find Press Ctrl + F . Type qty . Matches are highlighted as you type. Press Enter repeatedly to step through them. Press Esc to close the Find bar. Now press F3 . The search continues without the bar being open — and Shift + F3 goes back. Important F3 is the shortcut that saves the most time. Search once, then step through matches with one key while you edit. Whole word and case Search for item1 . Three matches — the declaration, the assignment and the Print. Search for item . Many more, because it matches inside item1 , item2 and item3 . Turn on Whole word and search for item again. No matches, because no variable is called exactly that. That is the difference in a nutshell, and it is the setting most often responsible for a Replace All going wrong. Replace Press Ctrl + H . Find qty , replace with quantity . Press Replace once. One occurrence changes and the next is found. Press Replace All . Everything else changes at once. Press Ctrl + Z . The whole Replace All is undone in one step. Tip Replace All is one undo away, but check Whole word first. Replacing id with identifier without it will happily rewrite the middle of every word containing id . Find in Project Press Ctrl + Shift + F . Search for Print . The Output panel opens on its Search results tab, listing every match in every file of the workspace. Click a result. That file opens with the caret on the line. With only two or three files this seems like overkill. On a real project it is how you answer \"where is this used?\" in a couple of seconds. A regular expression Open Find and turn on Regular expression . Search for ^Print — the caret means start of line . Only the lines beginning with Print match. Now search for \\d+ — one or more digits. Every number in the file matches. Turn the option off again when you have finished. Note Leaving Regular expression on catches people out later, when an "},{"u":"lesson-05.html","t":"Lesson 5 — Projects","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 5 project workspace save as project explorer categories add files","b":"Turn the files you already have into a named project, add files to it, and reopen it. Goal: Turn the files you already have into a named project, add files to it, and reopen it. Time: 15 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–4. What you will learn Why every workspace is already a project. Naming a workspace with Save as Project. How files are categorised in the Explorer. Why closing a tab does not remove a file from the project. Important Before you start, understand the central idea: in Tiko you already have a project . The files you have been editing form an untitled one. This lesson gives it a name. Look at what you have Press Ctrl + F4 to show the Explorer. You will see the category headers — Main, Modules, Headers, Resource, Other — with your files under them. At the top there is a pinned Save as Project… row. That row is telling you the workspace has no name yet. Name the project Click Save as Project… , or choose Project › Save Project As… . Save it as tutorial.tiko in your tutorial folder. The pinned row disappears and the window caption now names the project. Note Nothing about your files changed. The same files are in the same workspace — it now has a name, appears in Recent Projects, and can be reopened deliberately. Add a file Press Ctrl + N to create a new file. Type the code below and save it as helpers.bi in the same folder. fb helpers.bi Copy ' helpers.bi - Lesson 5 #pragma once Declare Function Describe( ByVal qty As Integer ) As String Function Describe( ByVal qty As Integer ) As String If qty = 0 Then Return \"out of stock\" If qty < 100 Then Return \"low\" Return \"plenty\" End Function Look at the Explorer. helpers.bi has been filed under Headers, because Tiko categorises by extension. Use it Edit inventory.bas so it includes and calls the helper: fb inventory.bas Copy ' inventory.bas - Lesson 5 #include once \"helpers.bi\" Dim As String item1, item2, item3 Dim As Integer qty1, qty2, qty3 item1 = \"bolt\" : qty1 = 100 item2 = \"nut\" : qty2 = 250 item3 = \"washer\": qty3 = 75 Print item1, qty1, Describe( qty1 ) Print item2, qty2, Describe( qty2 ) Print item3, qty3, Describe( qty3 ) Sleep Closing a tab is not removing a file Close helpers.bi with Ctrl + W . Look at the Explorer. The file is still listed. Click it. It opens again. Important A tab is a view of a file. Closing the tab stops displaying the file; it does not take it out of the project. It is "},{"u":"lesson-06.html","t":"Lesson 6 — Building a program","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 6 build compile run errors compiler setup quick run f5","b":"Configure the compiler, build and run your project, and navigate compiler errors. Goal: Configure the compiler, build and run your project, and navigate compiler errors. Time: 20 minutes Before you start: Lesson 5, plus a FreeBASIC compiler. What you will learn Setting the compiler path. Building and running with one keystroke. Reading build output and jumping to an error. When to use Compile, Run Executable and Quick Run. Note This lesson needs a FreeBASIC compiler installed. If you do not have one, skip ahead to Lesson 8 and come back later. Point Tiko at the compiler Open File › Settings › Options… . Select the Compiler page. Browse to your FreeBASIC installation and select the compiler executable. Choose OK . This is a one-off. Tiko remembers it for every project. Build and run Make inventory.bas the active document. Press F5 . The Output panel opens on its Compiler tab and shows the build. Your program runs in a console window. Type nothing — it prints and waits for a key. Press a key to close it. That single keystroke saved your files, compiled the project and ran the result. Break it deliberately Errors are more instructive than successes, so introduce one: In inventory.bas , change Describe( qty1 ) to Descrbe( qty1 ) . Press F5 . The build fails, and the Output panel lists the error. Click the error row. Tiko jumps to the exact line. Fix the typo and press F5 again. It builds. Important Always fix the first error and rebuild. One early mistake commonly produces a cascade of later errors that disappear on their own once it is fixed. The other build commands Press Ctrl + F5 — Compile. It builds but does not run. Press Shift + F5 — Run Executable. It runs the build you just made, without recompiling. Press Ctrl + Shift + F5 — Quick Run. It compiles and runs the current file on its own, ignoring the project. Tip Quick Run makes Tiko a good scratchpad. Open a new file, type ten lines to test a language feature, and press Ctrl + Shift + F5 — no project needed. See the command line Choose Compile › Command Line… to see exactly what Tiko passes to the compiler. When a build does something unexpected, this usually explains it in one glance. Expected result The program compiles and runs, printing three lines with a description beside each quantity, and you have navigated to a compiler error by clicking it. Common mistakes Symptom Cause and fix The build fails "},{"u":"lesson-07.html","t":"Lesson 7 — Debugging","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 7 debug breakpoint step over step into watch call stack locals","b":"Stop your program at a breakpoint, step through it, and inspect variables with watches and the call stack. Goal: Stop your program at a breakpoint, step through it, and inspect variables with watches and the call stack. Time: 20 minutes Before you start: Lesson 6. What you will learn Setting a breakpoint and starting a session. The difference between Step, Step Over and Step Out. Reading the Locals, Globals, Call stack and Watch panes. Adding watch expressions and spotting changed values. Debugging is how you find out what your program is actually doing, rather than what you believe it is doing. Give it something worth watching first. Replace inventory.bas with this loop version: fb inventory.bas Copy ' inventory.bas - Lesson 7 #include once \"helpers.bi\" Dim As String items(1 To 3) = { \"bolt\", \"nut\", \"washer\" } Dim As Integer qty(1 To 3) = { 100, 250, 75 } Dim As Integer total = 0 Dim As Integer i For i = 1 To 3 total += qty(i) Print items(i), qty(i), Describe( qty(i) ) Next i Print \"Total:\", total Sleep Set a breakpoint and start Put the caret on the total += qty(i) line. Press F9 . A marker appears in the margin. Press F6 to start debugging. The program builds, starts and stops on your breakpoint. The current line is marked. Note If the breakpoint jumps to the next line when you set it, that is deliberate: the line you chose generates no executable code, so Tiko moved it somewhere it can actually stop. Look at the state With the program stopped, four panes tell you where you are: Locals shows i , total and the arrays. i should be 1 and total 0. Call stack shows the chain of calls that reached this line. Hover over qty in the editor to see a data tip. Expand the qty array with its twisty to see all three elements. Step, and watch it change Press F10 (Step Over). The line executes and total becomes 100 — shown in a different colour, because it changed. Press F10 a few more times to go round the loop. Watch i and total change on each pass. When you reach the Describe(...) call, press F11 (Step) instead. You are now inside Describe , in helpers.bi . Look at the call stack: Describe sits above the main program. Press Shift + F11 (Step Out) to return to the caller. Important That is the whole distinction. F11 goes into a call; F10 runs it and stops after it. Use F10 by default. Add a watch In the Watch pane, click <click to add a watch> . Type total and press En"},{"u":"lesson-08.html","t":"Lesson 8 — Customization","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 8 customization theme font shortcuts settings options","b":"Choose a theme and font, enable the display aids you like, and remap a keyboard shortcut. Goal: Choose a theme and font, enable the display aids you like, and remap a keyboard shortcut. Time: 15 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–3. No compiler needed. What you will learn Switching themes and what a theme covers. Setting the editor font, size and line spacing. Why zoom and font size are different things. Remapping and resetting a keyboard shortcut. Which files hold your configuration. Try the themes Press Ctrl + Shift + T , or choose File › Settings › Themes… . Try several themes — a dark one, a light one, a high-contrast one. Settle on one you like and choose OK . Notice that the whole application changed, not just the code area: the menus, panels, tabs and status bar all follow the theme. Set the font Open File › Settings › Options… and go to the font settings. Change the size from 11 to 13. Choose OK and see the difference. Try a different monospaced font if you have one installed. Set extra line spacing to 3 and see how much easier dense code is to read. Important Use a monospaced font. Column selection, indent guides and the right-edge marker all assume every character is the same width. Zoom is not the font size Press Ctrl + + three times, then Ctrl + - three times. Press Ctrl + 0 to reset. Zoom is temporary and per-session; the font setting is permanent. Use zoom when you want a quick overview or need to read something closely, and the font setting for how you work all day. Turn on the display aids Open the options dialog and enable these, then look at your code: Option What you will see Indent guides Faint vertical lines at each indent level. Highlight current line The caret's line tinted. Right edge A vertical line at column 80. Brace highlight Matching parentheses highlighted as you move past them. Occurrence highlight Every other use of the word under the caret marked. Keep the ones that help and turn off the ones that distract. There is no wrong answer. Remap a shortcut Press Ctrl + K to open the Keyboard Shortcuts dialog. Type duplicate in the filter box to find Duplicate Line. Select it and choose Modify . Press Ctrl + Shift + D in the capture field. Choose OK , then OK again. Test it in the editor. Then reopen the dialog, select the command and choose Reset to restore Ctrl + D . Note Try assigning a keystroke that is already in use — Ctrl + S"},{"u":"lesson-09.html","t":"Lesson 9 — Productivity shortcuts","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 9 navigation goto definition search symbol autocomplete bookmarks formatter","b":"Navigate by symbol rather than by scrolling, and use autocomplete, bookmarks and the formatter. Goal: Navigate by symbol rather than by scrolling, and use autocomplete, bookmarks and the formatter. Time: 15 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–5. What you will learn Goto Definition and the navigation history. Finding any symbol in the project by name. Using autocomplete and reading code tips. Setting and cycling through bookmarks. Reformatting code safely. This lesson is about speed. Everything here replaces something you can already do slowly. Goto Definition and back Open inventory.bas and put the caret on Describe . Press F12 . Tiko opens helpers.bi at the function's definition. Press Alt + ← . You are back where you started. Press Alt + → . Forward again. Important F12 then Alt + ← is the core navigation loop — dive in, read, come back. If you learn one thing from this lesson, learn this pair. Search Symbol Press Ctrl + P . Type desc . The list narrows to matching symbols. Choose Describe and you jump straight to it — without knowing or caring which file it is in. The function list Press F4 to show the function list. Open helpers.bi and see Describe listed. Click it to jump there. On a file with thirty procedures this replaces a great deal of scrolling. Autocomplete and code tips In inventory.bas , start a new line and type Desc . The completion list appears. Press Tab to accept Describe . Type ( . A code tip appears showing the parameter list. Type qty(1)) and the tip closes. Delete the experimental line. Tip Typing ( also accepts the highlighted completion — so Desc followed by ( completes the name, inserts the parenthesis and shows the parameters in one keystroke. Bookmarks Put the caret on the For line and press Ctrl + F2 . Put the caret on the final Print and press Ctrl + F2 again. Press F2 repeatedly to cycle between them. Press Shift + F4 to see them listed in the side panel. Press Ctrl + Shift + F2 to clear them. The formatter Deliberately mangle the indentation of a few lines in inventory.bas . Press Shift + Alt + F (Format Document). The indentation is repaired. Press Ctrl + Z . The format is undone in one step, and your typing is preserved. Open Edit › Format › Format Options… . Turn on Case keywords and watch the live preview change. Choose Cancel to leave your settings as they were. Note The formatter never moves a line break. It will not tur"},{"u":"lesson-10.html","t":"Lesson 10 — Advanced editing","s":"Tutorial","k":"tutorial lesson 10 advanced split view folding multiple selections user tools todo","b":"Split views, code folding, multiple selections, user tools and the TODO list. Goal: Split views, code folding, multiple selections, user tools and the TODO list. Time: 20 minutes Before you start: Lessons 1–9. What you will learn Viewing two parts of one file at once. Folding code to see a file's structure. Editing several unrelated places at once. Defining an external tool. Using TODO comments as a shared task list. Split the editor Open inventory.bas . Press Ctrl + Shift + \\ to split top and bottom. Scroll one view to the top and the other to the bottom. You are looking at two parts of the same file at once. Type in one view and watch the change appear in the other — it is one document, not a copy. Press Ctrl + Shift + \\ again to return to a single view. Try Ctrl + \\ for a left/right split. Fold the code Press Shift + F8 (Fold All). Every block collapses and you see the file's outline. Press F8 on a folded block to expand just that one. Press Ctrl + Shift + F8 to expand everything. Tip Fold All is a good way to get your bearings in an unfamiliar file — you see the structure before the detail. Multiple selections Hold Ctrl and double-click qty in three different places. You now have three independent selections. Type count . All three change at once. Press Ctrl + Z to undo, and click once to collapse to a single caret. Multiple selections need not line up, which is what makes them different from column mode. Use column mode when things do line up, and this when they do not. Define a user tool Open File › User Tools… and choose Add . Description: Open project folder . Program: explorer.exe . Parameters: the substitution code for the current file's folder — the Parameters field's tooltip lists the codes available. Choose OK , then run it from File › User Tools . TODO — supply the exact substitution code Insert the exact parameter substitution code for the current file's directory once it has been confirmed against the shipping build. Use the Output panel's TODO tab Add a comment to helpers.bi : ' TODO: handle negative quantities . Open the Output panel ( Ctrl + F9 ) and select the TODO tab. Your comment is listed. Click it to jump there. TODO comments live in the source, so everyone who reads the code sees them — unlike bookmarks, which are yours alone. Take stock You have now used every major feature of the editor: Area Covered in Files and the interface Les"},{"u":"faq.html","t":"Frequently asked questions","s":"Help and Reference","k":"faq questions answers help common problems how do i","b":"Around sixty short answers to the questions that come up most often, from installation through to debugging and customization. Common questions, grouped roughly by subject. Use Ctrl + F to search this page, or Ctrl + K to search the whole documentation. How do I install Tiko? Unpack the distribution into a folder you can write to and run tiko.exe . There is no installer. Do not use C:\\Program Files — Tiko writes its settings beside the executable, and that folder is write-protected. Does Tiko need .NET or any other runtime? No. It is a native Win32 application. The DLLs it needs ship alongside it. Can I run Tiko from a USB stick? Yes. Nothing is stored in the registry or your user profile, so the folder is self-contained. See Settings and configuration files . Where does Tiko store my settings? In the settings folder beside tiko.exe — chiefly settings.ini and keybindings.ini . Is Tiko free? What licence is it under? Tiko is released under the GNU General Public License v3. See Help › About for the full text and credits. How do I enable dark mode? Press Ctrl + Shift + T to open the Themes dialog and choose a dark theme — default_dark , studio_dark , midnight and several others. The whole application follows, not just the editor. How do I change the font? File › Settings › Options… , then the font settings. Set the name, size, character set and extra line spacing. Use a monospaced font. The text is too small. Can I just make it bigger temporarily? Yes — Ctrl + + and Ctrl + - zoom, and Ctrl + 0 resets. Zoom does not change the configured font size. Can I make my own theme? Yes. Edit colours in the Themes dialog and save under a new name so an update cannot overwrite it. See The theme editor . How do I show line numbers? Turn on Line numbering in File › Settings › Options… . It is on by default. How do I show indent guides or a right-margin line? Both are options in File › Settings › Options… — Indent guides , and Right edge with Right edge position for the column. How do I edit several lines at once? Hold Alt and drag for a column selection, or hold Ctrl and click or drag to add independent selections. See Multiple selections and column mode . How do I comment out a block of code? Select the lines and press Ctrl + / . Ctrl + Shift + / uncomments. How do I move a line up or down? Alt + ↑ and Alt + ↓ . They move the whole selection if there is one. How do I delet"},{"u":"tips-and-tricks.html","t":"Tips and tricks","s":"Help and Reference","k":"tips tricks productivity power user workflow hidden features shortcuts habits","b":"Power-user habits, keyboard and mouse tricks, and the features people most often miss. Habits and shortcuts that repay the time it takes to learn them, roughly in order of how much difference they make. The five habits worth building first F12 then Alt + ← . Jump to a definition, read it, come straight back. This one pair replaces most scrolling and most file-switching. Search once, then F3 . Close the Find bar and step through matches with a single key while you edit. Ctrl + P for anything you can name. If you know what a procedure is called, you do not need to know where it lives. Cut and copy with nothing selected. Both act on the whole line, so moving a line is Ctrl + X , click, Ctrl + V . Shift + F8 before you read an unfamiliar file. Fold everything, see the structure, then expand what matters. Editing tricks Zero-width column selections insert. Alt -drag straight down without moving sideways to get one caret per line, then type once to prefix every line. Home is smart. It goes to the first non-blank character; press it again for column one. On indented code that is almost always what you meant. Ctrl + Enter opens a line below from anywhere on the current one. No need to press End first. Ctrl + L repeatedly extends the selection downward , so three presses selects three lines. Hold Alt + ↑ or Alt + ↓ to reorder several lines at once — select them first and the whole block moves. Paste is always plain text. Code copied from a web page or a document arrives without its formatting. Search tricks Select text before pressing Ctrl + F and it becomes the search term. Use Find in Project as a preview for Replace All. Search first, see how many places would change and whether they are all what you expect, then replace. Regular expressions for structural searches. ^\\s*(Sub|Function)\\s+\\w+ lists every procedure declaration in the project. Turn Regular expression off again when you are done. Leaving it on makes ordinary searches containing a full stop or a bracket behave strangely later. Navigation tricks Ctrl + Shift + H and Ctrl + Shift + C toggle between a module and its header — the pair you move between most while writing code. Ctrl + PgDn and Ctrl + PgUp step between procedures without leaving the keyboard. Bookmarks for now, TODO comments for later. Bookmarks are private and transient; TODO comments live in the source and are visible to everyone. The naviga"},{"u":"troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Help and Reference","k":"troubleshooting problems fix broken wont start compiler not found encoding slow performance search not working theme settings reset crash recovery","b":"Symptoms, causes and fixes for start-up, build, debugger, file, search, editing and layout problems. Symptoms, causes and fixes. Work down each list — the first item is the most common cause. Tiko will not start, or starts wrong Symptom Cause and fix It starts with no colours and the menus look wrong. You launched a copy of tiko.exe away from its settings folder. Run it from the folder you unpacked. It reports a missing DLL. The editing, parser or debugger DLLs are not beside the executable. Restore the full distribution. Settings are forgotten every time. Tiko cannot write to its own folder. Move it out of C:\\Program Files , or grant write permission. The window opens off screen or at a strange size. Stored geometry from another monitor arrangement. Choose View › Restore Main Window Size , or close Tiko and delete the [Startup] geometry keys from settings.ini . A change I made in the options had no effect. You may have two installations and be launching the other one. Each folder has its own settings — check which tiko.exe you ran. Compiler and build problems Symptom Cause and fix “Compiler not found”, or the build does nothing. The compiler path is unset or wrong. Set it in File › Settings › Options… on the Compiler page. The build fails with errors that make no sense. Fix the first error and rebuild. One early mistake commonly produces a cascade. The build succeeds but runs old behaviour. A file was not saved. Enable Compile autosave . Builds behave inconsistently after changing switches. Stale object files. Use Rebuild All ( Ctrl + Alt + F5 ). The program window flashes and disappears. Your program ended immediately. Add Sleep at the end, or run it from a console. Linker errors about a library or an architecture mismatch. A 32-bit program cannot link a 64-bit library. Check which compiler Tiko is configured to use. Debugger problems Symptom Cause and fix Debugging will not start. The executable carries no debug information. Select a Debug build configuration and rebuild. Breakpoints are never hit. The code is not reached, or the executable is stale — rebuild. Confirm by putting a breakpoint on the first line of the program. A breakpoint moved when I set it. That is intended: the line generated no executable code, so it moved to the next line that did. Break does not stop the program. It stops at the next line of your source; a program blocked in a librar"},{"u":"glossary.html","t":"Glossary","s":"Help and Reference","k":"glossary terminology definitions terms vocabulary","b":"Plain-language definitions of the editing, project, build and debugging terms used across this documentation. Terms used throughout this documentation, in alphabetical order. Accelerator A keyboard shortcut that runs a command directly, such as Ctrl + S . See Keyboard shortcuts . ANSI The legacy single-byte Windows encoding for a locale. Adequate for plain ASCII, unreliable for anything else. See Encoding . Autocomplete A list of possible completions offered as you type an identifier. See Autocomplete and code tips . BOM (byte-order mark) A few bytes at the start of a file recording its encoding. Some tools require one; others cannot cope with it. Bookmark A marked line you can jump back to. Stored in the editor, not in the file. See Bookmarks . Breakpoint A marker telling the debugger to stop your program when execution reaches that line. See Breakpoints . Build configuration A named set of compiler switches, such as Debug or Release. See Build configurations . Call stack The chain of procedure calls that reached the current line, innermost first. See Watches and the call stack . Caret The blinking text insertion point. Distinct from the mouse cursor. Code tip A popup showing a procedure's parameters, displayed when you open its call parenthesis. Sometimes called a call tip. Column selection A rectangular selection covering the same columns on several lines. See Multiple selections and column mode . Compiler The program that turns source code into an executable. Tiko drives the FreeBASIC compiler; it does not include one. CRLF / LF / CR The three line-ending conventions: Windows, Unix and classic Mac OS. See Encoding and line endings . Data tip The value of a variable shown when you hover over it in the editor while debugging. Encoding The rule mapping bytes in a file to characters — UTF-8, ANSI and so on. Fold A collapsed block of code, shown as a single line. Folding is a display state and does not change the file. See Code folding . FreeBASIC The BASIC-dialect compiler Tiko is built around. Free, open source and self-hosting. Gutter / margin The narrow strips left of the text holding line numbers, bookmark and breakpoint markers, and fold markers. Help Center Tiko's built-in documentation browser, opened with F1 . Keyword A word with special meaning in a language, such as Dim or Function . Keyword lists live in settings\\keywords . Lexer The component tha"},{"u":"doc-index.html","t":"Index","s":"Help and Reference","k":"index alphabetical a-z topics list contents","b":"A complete alphabetical index of the topics and features covered in this documentation. Every topic and feature, alphabetically. Use Ctrl + K to search the whole documentation instead. A B C D E F G H I K L M N O P Q R S T U W Z A About dialog Accelerators Add files to project ANSI encoding Auto indentation Auto save Autocomplete B Backing up settings Block completion BOM (byte-order mark) Bookmarks Bookmarks list Brace matching Breadcrumbs Breakpoints Build and Execute Build configurations Build output C Call stack Caret movement Case conversion Character set Clipboard Close file Code folding Code formatting Code tips Column selection Command line (compiler) Command reference Comment block Compile Compiler errors Compiler setup Configuration reference Confine caret Context menus CRLF and LF Current line highlight D Data tips Debugging Delete line Dialog reference Dialogs Duplicate line E Encoding Explorer Exporting settings F FAQ Find Find in Project Find Next Fold All Fold margin Fonts Format Document Format on Enter Format Options dialog Format Project Function list G Glossary Go Back / Go Forward Goto Definition Goto Header File Goto Line H Help Center High-DPI displays Highlight occurrences I Indent guides Indentation Insert file Installation K Keybindings file Keyboard customization Keyboard shortcuts Keyword case Keyword lists L Language (interface) Language (syntax) Lexer Line endings Line numbers Line operations Localization M Main module Main window Margins Menu bar Menu reference Minimap Move line Multiple instances Multiple selections N Navigation Notes tab O Open file Open File As Template Open Recent Options dialog Output panel Overwrite mode P Panel icon strip Portable installation Printing this help Project Explorer Project options Projects Q Quick Run Quick Start R Rebuild All Recent Files Recent Projects Redo Regular expressions Rename file Replace Replace All Resetting settings Resource file Right edge marker Run executable Run to Cursor S Save / Save As Save as Project Scintilla Scrolling Search Symbol Selecting text Settings files Side panels Split views Status bar Stepping (debugger) Symbols Syntax highlighting System requirements T Tab size Tabs (documents) Tabs versus spaces Theme editor Themes Tips and tricks TODO comments Toolbar Troubleshooting Tutorial U Undo Unicode Unused Symbols User tools UTF-8 W Watches Whitespace display Who"}];
